00:00:21.423 --> 00:00:23.840
(slow music)

00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:28.028
- Hi, welcome to The
Leaders of Learning Podcast,

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:29.588
I'm your host, Ling Ling.

00:00:29.588 --> 00:00:32.545
Joining us is a very
special guest, Gary Vee.

00:00:32.545 --> 00:00:35.813
Gary Vee is a serial
entrepreneur, investor,

00:00:35.813 --> 00:00:38.548
a New York Times' bestseller,
what else am I missing, Gary?

00:00:38.548 --> 00:00:39.628
- Those were all plenty, thank you.

00:00:39.628 --> 00:00:40.461
- A whole bunch of stuff.

00:00:40.461 --> 00:00:42.003
- Thanks, Ling Ling, it's good to see you.

00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:43.573
- And all around, a really cool guy.

00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:44.406
- Thank you.

00:00:44.406 --> 00:00:45.518
- If you don't know him
already, please listen

00:00:45.518 --> 00:00:49.209
to this podcast episode, so
I've become an entrepreneur

00:00:49.209 --> 00:00:51.787
about two years ago, a
little baby compared to you,

00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:54.614
and of course it's a rollercoaster ride,

00:00:54.614 --> 00:00:56.499
as being an entrepreneur.

00:00:56.499 --> 00:00:59.275
And I believe a lot of my
listeners are also interested,

00:00:59.275 --> 00:01:01.486
or thinking about
starting their own thing,

00:01:01.486 --> 00:01:03.168
so I thought maybe I could take this time

00:01:03.168 --> 00:01:04.854
to help my listeners try to figure out--

00:01:04.854 --> 00:01:07.131
- Let's do it.
- How they start.

00:01:07.131 --> 00:01:09.274
So one of my questions
I have is, you know,

00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:10.766
what is the biggest thing you struggled

00:01:10.766 --> 00:01:15.300
with most as an entrepreneur?
- Not being able to be one.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.597
You know--
- What does that mean?

00:01:16.597 --> 00:01:20.057
- That means that the first 12 years

00:01:20.057 --> 00:01:24.634
of my school career were
genuinely, and I mean this,

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:28.194
the day I left college to go
work in my dad's liquor store,

00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:29.716
and really run it, at that point,

00:01:29.716 --> 00:01:33.377
because I'd grown up
in that store was the,

00:01:33.377 --> 00:01:36.045
I can remember almost
everything about my last day

00:01:36.045 --> 00:01:38.817
in college, driving down
Route 95 from Boston

00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:41.650
to New Jersey, and that afternoon,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.199
at, like, 6:13, pulling up,

00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:48.193
it's funny that I am that
specific with my time,

00:01:48.193 --> 00:01:51.614
but I remember looking at the car clock.

00:01:51.614 --> 00:01:56.054
I spent an hour working in
the store before we went home,

00:01:56.054 --> 00:01:57.911
and that's, like, literally
how my career started.

00:01:57.911 --> 00:02:01.214
Like, I loved it so
much that after driving

00:02:01.214 --> 00:02:05.685
for seven hours, 'cause there
was a lot of traffic that day,

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:09.150
from college, that I
wanted to work an hour

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.484
in the liquor store to,
like, start my career.

00:02:12.484 --> 00:02:13.642
So the hardest thing for me,

00:02:13.642 --> 00:02:15.429
and there's a very specific
reason I'm saying this

00:02:15.429 --> 00:02:17.334
to everybody, the hardest thing for me,

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:20.499
being an entrepreneur, has been not much

00:02:20.499 --> 00:02:23.125
because it is truly, truly my place.

00:02:23.125 --> 00:02:27.441
And I do believe that
self-awareness is very important,

00:02:27.441 --> 00:02:31.614
and so it's hard for me to
think about the negativities

00:02:31.614 --> 00:02:34.667
of entrepreneurship, because
I just genuinely love it

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:37.315
so much, I love when I lose a client,

00:02:37.315 --> 00:02:40.667
I love when somebody quits,
I love when somebody steals

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.359
from me, and I know this sounds weird.

00:02:43.359 --> 00:02:45.358
And obviously I don't love
when somebody steals from me,

00:02:45.358 --> 00:02:47.990
but I'm so grateful to genuinely be doing

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:51.014
what I was meant to do,
that it's hard for me

00:02:51.014 --> 00:02:54.511
to get going, and, look,
entrepreneurship is lonely.

00:02:54.511 --> 00:02:58.002
And I think a lot of
people, I always wonder,

00:02:58.002 --> 00:03:02.345
I think people are a scale,
from zero to 100, in everything.

00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:05.428
Singing, looks, humor, patience,

00:03:05.428 --> 00:03:07.275
I think we go from zero to 100.

00:03:07.275 --> 00:03:09.461
I think when it comes to entrepreneurship,

00:03:09.461 --> 00:03:12.345
I really genuinely think I'm 100.

00:03:12.345 --> 00:03:15.481
I struggle with any of the negativities.

00:03:15.481 --> 00:03:17.840
You know, I say that
entrepreneurship is lonely,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.524
'cause it is, but I like being lonely.

00:03:20.524 --> 00:03:24.912
I like being on a 17 hour
flight, like I was today,

00:03:24.912 --> 00:03:29.246
and thinking for two, three
hours about only negative stuff,

00:03:29.246 --> 00:03:31.159
stuff that most people don't
want to be thinking about,

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:33.658
this problem, that problem.

00:03:33.658 --> 00:03:34.562
So that's my story.
- Yeah.

00:03:34.562 --> 00:03:36.826
I absolutely agree with
you about being very lonely

00:03:36.826 --> 00:03:38.151
in the entrepreneurial journey

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.073
because everyone has their own ideas,

00:03:40.073 --> 00:03:42.041
they want to execute it, but to get people

00:03:42.041 --> 00:03:44.168
to follow along with you is not as easy.

00:03:44.168 --> 00:03:45.918
So if any of our listeners would like

00:03:45.918 --> 00:03:48.774
to start their own business,
or become an entrepreneur,

00:03:48.774 --> 00:03:50.166
what are the key things they need

00:03:50.166 --> 00:03:52.607
to consider first before
they embark on that journey?

00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:55.580
- Right now, entrepreneurship
is cool, it's flashy,

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.274
it's exciting, and so the
first thing I think they need

00:03:58.274 --> 00:04:00.878
to think about is, look, if
you're looking to sign up

00:04:00.878 --> 00:04:03.516
for this, rest assure that the first five

00:04:03.516 --> 00:04:05.824
to six years are gonna suck.

00:04:05.824 --> 00:04:08.065
They're gonna be lonely,
they're gonna be difficult.

00:04:08.065 --> 00:04:10.862
What has made it less suck is,

00:04:10.862 --> 00:04:13.782
there's so much capital
being thrown at people.

00:04:13.782 --> 00:04:15.482
So this is, the reason
there's so many people

00:04:15.482 --> 00:04:18.377
in entrepreneurship
today is, it's very easy,

00:04:18.377 --> 00:04:22.198
and I'm gonna use that, it's
easy to raise a million dollars

00:04:22.198 --> 00:04:26.221
for your idea now, not the
case when I was growing up,

00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:28.272
not the case after the
economy's gonna collapse

00:04:28.272 --> 00:04:30.037
inevitably at some point.

00:04:30.037 --> 00:04:33.382
So what's happening is
people are getting tricked,

00:04:33.382 --> 00:04:35.158
'cause they're raising capital,

00:04:35.158 --> 00:04:36.470
and they think they're doing well,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.746
but they're burning
cash, and the reality is,

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:43.977
they're only in fundraising
mode at all times.

00:04:43.977 --> 00:04:46.716
And so, A, if they're thinking about it,

00:04:46.716 --> 00:04:48.647
recognize that if you're fortunate enough

00:04:48.647 --> 00:04:50.644
to be in a family, in a situation

00:04:50.644 --> 00:04:52.527
that's gonna allow you to raise capital,

00:04:52.527 --> 00:04:54.384
recognize that that is most likely going

00:04:54.384 --> 00:04:57.566
to be your vulnerability, it's
gonna be feel good up front,

00:04:57.566 --> 00:05:00.278
but on the backend, it's
gonna be your vulnerability.

00:05:00.278 --> 00:05:01.556
And for the people who are listening

00:05:01.556 --> 00:05:04.321
that don't have the
luxury of raising capital,

00:05:04.321 --> 00:05:06.595
recognize that you have an advantage.

00:05:06.595 --> 00:05:10.488
You're gonna be forced
to actually make money.

00:05:10.488 --> 00:05:12.562
And one of the things
I'm most proud of is,

00:05:12.562 --> 00:05:14.307
the two businesses that I've run,

00:05:14.307 --> 00:05:16.142
you know, there was no capital infusion.

00:05:16.142 --> 00:05:17.845
There wasn't a credit
line, or raising capital

00:05:17.845 --> 00:05:20.163
to build WineLibrary.com,
but there was a foundation

00:05:20.163 --> 00:05:21.748
of the business that my dad have.

00:05:21.748 --> 00:05:23.889
But VaynerMedia started in
a conference room like this,

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:27.122
with no capital, and I was always forced

00:05:27.122 --> 00:05:30.683
to make money, and I think
for anybody who's listening,

00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:33.565
instead of thinking about raising capital,

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:35.118
you know, why don't you
try to figure out how

00:05:35.118 --> 00:05:37.787
to make money, but if that's the case,

00:05:37.787 --> 00:05:39.248
recognize it's gonna take a half decade

00:05:39.248 --> 00:05:40.739
of a lot of grinding.

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:43.161
- Yeah, I remember listening
to a talk somewhere,

00:05:43.161 --> 00:05:45.091
one of the local entrepreneurs
said the best money

00:05:45.091 --> 00:05:46.744
to earn is the money from your clients,

00:05:46.744 --> 00:05:48.244
and not from your investors,

00:05:48.244 --> 00:05:51.323
and that kind of just blew my mind away.

00:05:51.323 --> 00:05:53.139
It's like, oh, yeah, that's really true,

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:55.958
'cause in a way, it's a
validation from clients, right?

00:05:55.958 --> 00:05:58.885
If they're willing to pay
for your services or product.

00:05:58.885 --> 00:06:00.690
- Oh, gosh, I mean, I couldn't agree more

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.430
with whoever said that, I mean, to me,

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.841
this will be known as the great era

00:06:05.841 --> 00:06:08.746
of people building financial
arbitrage machines,

00:06:08.746 --> 00:06:12.167
not companies, people are building decks,

00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:15.011
and taking meetings with BCs all day,

00:06:15.011 --> 00:06:17.389
they're not worried about their product.

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:18.767
I mean, I don't know what else to say,

00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:21.268
like, I mean, I just,
there's an enormous amount

00:06:21.268 --> 00:06:23.315
of bad companies in the world today,

00:06:23.315 --> 00:06:24.553
because there's so much capital.

00:06:24.553 --> 00:06:27.303
And that will eventually go away.

00:06:28.278 --> 00:06:31.193
- Indeed, you mentioned earlier about,

00:06:31.193 --> 00:06:33.780
the biggest challenge is
not being an entrepreneur.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.500
Do you believe it's,
entrepreneur is something

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.108
that you're born with, passionate,

00:06:37.108 --> 00:06:38.791
or is it something that can be learned?

00:06:38.791 --> 00:06:41.388
And if it can be learned, what are the

00:06:41.388 --> 00:06:42.698
characteristics of an entrepreneur

00:06:42.698 --> 00:06:44.797
that can make them successful?

00:06:44.797 --> 00:06:46.992
- I believe that
entrepreneurship is similar

00:06:46.992 --> 00:06:51.992
to dancing and singing and
playing ping pong and basketball.

00:06:52.372 --> 00:06:55.522
What that means is, it can be taught.

00:06:55.522 --> 00:06:59.039
The problem is, being a
successful entrepreneur means

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:00.652
that you how to be good.

00:07:00.652 --> 00:07:02.213
- What does that mean, good?

00:07:02.213 --> 00:07:04.237
- Good means you need
to be able to sustain,

00:07:04.237 --> 00:07:06.925
and make money, and
not go out of business,

00:07:06.925 --> 00:07:09.568
and I don't think everybody
is capable of that.

00:07:09.568 --> 00:07:10.573
- Okay.

00:07:10.573 --> 00:07:13.434
- I don't think that, you know,
most people are not capable

00:07:13.434 --> 00:07:17.450
of getting paid to sing,
forget about being Beyonce,

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:21.689
or Lady Gaga, I mean, can
you make money singing

00:07:21.689 --> 00:07:25.693
on a cruise ship, or in a
bar, or playing local pubs

00:07:25.693 --> 00:07:28.028
to sustain your lifestyle?

00:07:28.028 --> 00:07:32.811
It's difficult, and I think
most people are not capable

00:07:32.811 --> 00:07:34.488
of building an entrepreneurial business

00:07:34.488 --> 00:07:36.276
that sustains their life.

00:07:36.276 --> 00:07:38.532
- But it also sounds like, to
be a successful entrepreneur,

00:07:38.532 --> 00:07:40.727
you need to be first and
foremost a salesperson,

00:07:40.727 --> 00:07:42.726
and not just, or singing, or dancing,

00:07:42.726 --> 00:07:44.441
'cause people who are singing or dancing,

00:07:44.441 --> 00:07:47.115
they love doing that, right?
- Yeah.

00:07:47.115 --> 00:07:49.628
- But to get money, you need
to have sales skills, no?

00:07:49.628 --> 00:07:52.439
- Yeah, yes, I mean, you know, obviously,

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:55.856
generating revenue is part of it, and so,

00:07:57.362 --> 00:07:59.778
and there are a lot of
people who are not capable

00:07:59.778 --> 00:08:03.266
of selling, however, she or he can partner

00:08:03.266 --> 00:08:07.485
with a salesperson, or
hire one, and, you know,

00:08:07.485 --> 00:08:10.709
and, you know, if you're
the greatest maker

00:08:10.709 --> 00:08:12.955
of putting in the world,

00:08:12.955 --> 00:08:15.088
and you're gonna have the
best product in the world,

00:08:15.088 --> 00:08:17.326
and you have a secret recipe
from your grandmother,

00:08:17.326 --> 00:08:19.953
and you're a hustler, and
you want to make a pudding,

00:08:19.953 --> 00:08:22.724
but you're petrified to
sell to people, you partner

00:08:22.724 --> 00:08:25.767
with somebody, or you hire
somebody after you raise capital,

00:08:25.767 --> 00:08:28.111
or start selling a little bit at first.

00:08:28.111 --> 00:08:31.611
So yes, I do think that a singular person,

00:08:32.662 --> 00:08:35.370
if they have no partnerships
in the beginning,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.855
needs to understand how
to generate revenue.

00:08:38.855 --> 00:08:41.486
I think these things are teachable,

00:08:41.486 --> 00:08:44.080
but I think people need to
know that they have a ceiling,

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.125
'cause entrepreneurship is a skill,

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:48.395
and in any skill, we have a ceiling.

00:08:48.395 --> 00:08:50.694
I can become a much
better painter tomorrow

00:08:50.694 --> 00:08:52.884
than I am today, I just don't think

00:08:52.884 --> 00:08:55.788
I'm going to be an all-time painter.

00:08:55.788 --> 00:08:57.102
- Okay, so it's just understanding

00:08:57.102 --> 00:08:58.837
and recognizing one's own limits

00:08:58.837 --> 00:09:00.533
on how much you want to do?

00:09:00.533 --> 00:09:02.457
- And you know what's
funny, it's not that I want

00:09:02.457 --> 00:09:05.383
to contain things into limits,
it's that I want people

00:09:05.383 --> 00:09:09.162
to start realizing
entrepreneurship is a talent.

00:09:09.162 --> 00:09:10.956
Everybody just thinks,
like, you say you're one,

00:09:10.956 --> 00:09:13.144
and away you go, and they would never do

00:09:13.144 --> 00:09:15.337
that with being a professional skier,

00:09:15.337 --> 00:09:17.508
or a professional three star chef.

00:09:17.508 --> 00:09:20.417
But for some reason, the
nature of business is

00:09:20.417 --> 00:09:24.716
so inclusive, which is
amazing, the problem is,

00:09:24.716 --> 00:09:29.064
it got so cool that now
we have a whole generation

00:09:29.064 --> 00:09:31.224
of people who are forcing themselves

00:09:31.224 --> 00:09:35.537
to be entrepreneurs to fit
in that just don't have it.

00:09:35.537 --> 00:09:38.047
And that's gonna lead
to a very scary place,

00:09:38.047 --> 00:09:41.881
because what's very amazing
about entrepreneurship is

00:09:41.881 --> 00:09:44.974
that it's inclusive,
what's very devastating

00:09:44.974 --> 00:09:48.234
about entrepreneurship
is it's not forgiving.

00:09:48.234 --> 00:09:49.810
- Why isn't it forgiving?

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.315
- Because when you lose,
you're out of business,

00:09:51.315 --> 00:09:54.150
and everybody knows, you
know, your parents know,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.233
the world knows, and you've failed.

00:09:57.233 --> 00:10:01.991
And that is tough, because when
you're not an entrepreneur,

00:10:01.991 --> 00:10:04.515
failure is scary.
- Yeah.

00:10:04.515 --> 00:10:08.068
- And so that framework
leads to depression

00:10:08.068 --> 00:10:10.152
and unhappiness, and that needs to be

00:10:10.152 --> 00:10:12.034
of the ambition of human beings,

00:10:12.034 --> 00:10:15.254
and so I'm a little bit worried
that we've over-corrected.

00:10:15.254 --> 00:10:17.792
I'm thrilled that
entrepreneurship is now viable,

00:10:17.792 --> 00:10:20.469
and it's not just, go to
college and get a job.

00:10:20.469 --> 00:10:22.687
I'm a little concerned
that we've over-corrected,

00:10:22.687 --> 00:10:23.834
and we have a great generation

00:10:23.834 --> 00:10:25.269
of people who are not prepared

00:10:25.269 --> 00:10:28.953
for the ultimate loss
of public humiliation,

00:10:28.953 --> 00:10:31.513
because they're talking a big game now,

00:10:31.513 --> 00:10:34.361
I'm gonna build a big
company, di di di di di di di,

00:10:34.361 --> 00:10:37.607
and when it fails, it's gonna hurt.

00:10:37.607 --> 00:10:39.702
- Some people, they are so ingrained

00:10:39.702 --> 00:10:42.400
in the idea of, my idea
of being a business

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:44.250
and an entrepreneur, and
they think it's really big,

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.609
it's gonna change the world, but how would

00:10:45.609 --> 00:10:48.033
you know if you're ready
for the big humiliation

00:10:48.033 --> 00:10:50.063
that you're talking about?

00:10:50.063 --> 00:10:51.564
'Cause some people are just so big

00:10:51.564 --> 00:10:54.120
in their ego, I think my
idea is great and fantastic,

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.605
and they put a lot of effort in it, and--

00:10:57.605 --> 00:11:00.048
- You know, I think anybody
who's listening right now,

00:11:00.048 --> 00:11:02.014
if you want to set
yourself up for success,

00:11:02.014 --> 00:11:04.036
ask yourself how much risk you've taken

00:11:04.036 --> 00:11:07.185
in the first 10, 15, 20,
30, 40 years of your life.

00:11:07.185 --> 00:11:10.018
Ask yourself how much
you've been succumbing

00:11:10.018 --> 00:11:13.120
to peer pressure, like, if
you've always wanted to be cool

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.642
to the popular kids in your
high school, you're in trouble,

00:11:15.642 --> 00:11:17.508
because that means you're
pandering to a system.

00:11:17.508 --> 00:11:19.456
So, you know, I don't
know if anybody knows

00:11:19.456 --> 00:11:20.631
what they're prepared for,

00:11:20.631 --> 00:11:21.783
I think people are way more prepared

00:11:21.783 --> 00:11:25.799
for things than they
realize, on the flip side.

00:11:25.799 --> 00:11:27.766
But yeah, I mean, that's
the other thing right now.

00:11:27.766 --> 00:11:30.257
We have these spectacular companies,

00:11:30.257 --> 00:11:33.563
like Uber and Facebook, and WeChat,

00:11:33.563 --> 00:11:35.990
and all these companies
that make everybody think

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:38.735
you have to make a game
changing, you know,

00:11:38.735 --> 00:11:40.738
technology, or things of that nature.

00:11:40.738 --> 00:11:45.043
The reality, though, is 99% of people

00:11:45.043 --> 00:11:45.876
should

00:11:46.883 --> 00:11:48.415
have aspirations to build a business

00:11:48.415 --> 00:11:52.386
that does 500,000 a year in
revenue, not 500 trillion,

00:11:52.386 --> 00:11:55.006
but everybody's trying to
build the next Instagram,

00:11:55.006 --> 00:11:58.697
and I think that's going to be a problem.

00:11:58.697 --> 00:12:00.902
- Yikes, yikes, that's so scary.

00:12:00.902 --> 00:12:02.066
Well, talking about Instagram,

00:12:02.066 --> 00:12:06.201
you're also of this social
media mogul globally.

00:12:06.201 --> 00:12:08.626
What do you think, where
do you think Asia is going

00:12:08.626 --> 00:12:10.728
when it comes to the
social media landscape?

00:12:10.728 --> 00:12:12.319
Will we see more of the usual suspects,

00:12:12.319 --> 00:12:15.512
like Facebook, or Instagram,
or shall we see something

00:12:15.512 --> 00:12:18.495
a little bit more like
podcasting, for example?

00:12:18.495 --> 00:12:21.584
- So Asia's an interesting spot, right,

00:12:21.584 --> 00:12:23.938
because Mainland China, outside of Russia,

00:12:23.938 --> 00:12:26.615
is really one of the few places at scale,

00:12:26.615 --> 00:12:27.719
'cause obviously there's Line,

00:12:27.719 --> 00:12:30.364
and there's other,
Toutiao, there's things,

00:12:30.364 --> 00:12:32.539
but Mainland China is the one place,

00:12:32.539 --> 00:12:37.539
WeChat, Weibo, that has massive
scale in social networks.

00:12:37.753 --> 00:12:39.573
It's really been interesting for me

00:12:39.573 --> 00:12:43.997
to watch Southeast Asia
adapt, US platforms adapt,

00:12:43.997 --> 00:12:47.493
Chinese platforms, I actually
think Southeast Asia,

00:12:47.493 --> 00:12:49.834
as a whole, is one of
the most dynamic places

00:12:49.834 --> 00:12:52.202
for this conversation,
because they're influenced

00:12:52.202 --> 00:12:54.092
by both sides,

00:12:54.092 --> 00:12:56.458
more than anywhere else
I've seen in the world.

00:12:56.458 --> 00:12:57.291
So,

00:12:58.336 --> 00:13:02.365
you know, I view podcasting
as a medium in itself

00:13:02.365 --> 00:13:04.603
that is slightly different
than the way we think

00:13:04.603 --> 00:13:07.775
about social media, but
to your point, I do think

00:13:07.775 --> 00:13:10.823
that social media has become
a broadcasting platform,

00:13:10.823 --> 00:13:13.541
which is no different than podcasting.

00:13:13.541 --> 00:13:15.820
Look, I think humans want to communicate,

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:19.046
and they always have, and so
whether it's written word,

00:13:19.046 --> 00:13:21.710
audio, or video, that is
what we will always do.

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:26.449
And so I think cultural,
contextual nuances always play

00:13:26.449 --> 00:13:29.361
throughout the world,
we've seen it in cuisine

00:13:29.361 --> 00:13:34.361
and in fashion, and we will
see it in technology behaviors.

00:13:34.678 --> 00:13:37.295
But I think the opportunity

00:13:37.295 --> 00:13:39.660
has never been greater for human beings

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:43.964
to take advantage of
opportunities in front of them

00:13:43.964 --> 00:13:46.373
because of the scale of these platforms.

00:13:46.373 --> 00:13:50.323
And obviously, every
country has its own dynamics

00:13:50.323 --> 00:13:51.823
of censorship, or,

00:13:52.853 --> 00:13:56.109
you know, it's, I think
Americans are very naive.

00:13:56.109 --> 00:13:59.044
They cast judgment on
places like China or Russia,

00:13:59.044 --> 00:14:00.965
but we have our own
versions of it in America,

00:14:00.965 --> 00:14:02.043
it just plays out differently.

00:14:02.043 --> 00:14:03.854
And so there's a lot
of different sandboxes,

00:14:03.854 --> 00:14:05.340
a lot of different opportunities.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.660
Some places have more
opportunities than others,

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:09.561
some are rigged games more than others,

00:14:09.561 --> 00:14:12.698
but the reality is, it's a special time.

00:14:12.698 --> 00:14:16.159
- So just broadcast, use
whatever medium, depending on--

00:14:16.159 --> 00:14:17.605
- Yeah, the good--
- What you want to do?

00:14:17.605 --> 00:14:19.022
- The fun thing here is, look,

00:14:19.022 --> 00:14:21.115
we don't have to predict the next medium,

00:14:21.115 --> 00:14:24.584
we just need to properly
use the medium of today.

00:14:24.584 --> 00:14:25.542
You know, a lot of people are like,

00:14:25.542 --> 00:14:28.049
"Gary Vee, what's next,"
I'm like, I have no idea.

00:14:28.049 --> 00:14:30.357
I've never, ever predicted anything,

00:14:30.357 --> 00:14:34.774
I've just been the fastest
to move on everything.

00:14:34.774 --> 00:14:36.480
- I see, I see.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:37.751
So if,

00:14:37.751 --> 00:14:40.663
if you could offer a first
time entrepreneur one piece

00:14:40.663 --> 00:14:43.600
of advice, what would that be?

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.945
- Don't listen to a soul.

00:14:45.945 --> 00:14:47.617
Don't listen to the positive, don't listen

00:14:47.617 --> 00:14:51.152
to the negative, don't, you
listen to too much positive,

00:14:51.152 --> 00:14:54.609
you become entitled and soft, you listen

00:14:54.609 --> 00:14:57.758
to too much negative, you
become weak and scared.

00:14:57.758 --> 00:15:01.663
Don't listen to anything,
listen to your consumer.

00:15:01.663 --> 00:15:06.663
Not the headlines, not your
parents, just your consumer.

00:15:07.156 --> 00:15:08.547
- Fantastic, Gary, thank you so much

00:15:08.547 --> 00:15:10.348
for being on the show,
it's such a pleasure to--

00:15:10.348 --> 00:15:11.806
- Thanks for having me.

00:15:11.806 --> 00:15:14.473
