with Gary, I'm gonna take

00:18:14.684 --> 00:18:18.674
you off the speaker so
it's clear, here's Gary.

00:18:18.674 --> 00:18:19.841
- How are you?

00:18:22.918 --> 00:18:27.562
I got in a couple hours, I
got in a couple hours ago.

00:18:27.562 --> 00:18:31.979
And meetings and meetings,
and we're off and running.

00:18:34.272 --> 00:18:35.522
Happy to do it.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.673
- [Martha] Yeah. (mumbles)

00:18:46.342 --> 00:18:47.994
- You know, it's something, you know,

00:18:47.994 --> 00:18:50.699
I grew up in the wine business,
since I was 14 years old,

00:18:50.699 --> 00:18:52.971
so I'm sure, you know, it's
always subconsciously been

00:18:52.971 --> 00:18:56.156
in my mind, of, like, will
I ever have my own brand?

00:18:56.156 --> 00:18:59.262
It started getting more
serious out of circumstances,

00:18:59.262 --> 00:19:02.490
meaning, my two partners, John and Nate,

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:06.247
have worked for me and
with me for 10 years or so,

00:19:06.247 --> 00:19:08.476
and it, you know, there was a crossroads

00:19:08.476 --> 00:19:11.021
in their careers at VaynerMedia,

00:19:11.021 --> 00:19:14.617
and the huge emergence of
direct-to-consumer brands,

00:19:14.617 --> 00:19:17.320
as a trend that I was looking at.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.807
And, you know, being out
of day-to-day wine business

00:19:20.807 --> 00:19:22.989
for the last seven or
eight years, just a lot

00:19:22.989 --> 00:19:25.248
of different things
happened that made me say,

00:19:25.248 --> 00:19:27.868
hey, what would it look like
if I started my own brand?

00:19:27.868 --> 00:19:30.800
And that's kind of how it started.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:34.383
- [Martha] Okay. (mumbles)

00:19:40.589 --> 00:19:42.541
- You know, John and Nate were easy

00:19:42.541 --> 00:19:44.298
because they came to me as interns

00:19:44.298 --> 00:19:46.074
when I was doing the wine business.

00:19:46.074 --> 00:19:50.388
So they, you know, they were
there along the whole way.

00:19:50.388 --> 00:19:53.470
It was just a very obvious transition,

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:56.651
when I decided to do it, you know, again,

00:19:56.651 --> 00:19:59.435
I decided to do it because they had become

00:19:59.435 --> 00:20:02.431
very strong executives
at VaynerMedia, and--

00:20:02.431 --> 00:20:04.734
(Martha mumbles)

00:20:04.734 --> 00:20:08.304
Oh, really?
(Martha mumbles)

00:20:08.304 --> 00:20:10.804
No, no problem, I'm trying to,

00:20:12.324 --> 00:20:16.073
huh, I'm not moving,
maybe I'm in a bad spot?

00:20:16.073 --> 00:20:18.408
Maybe I'll just move
over here for a second.

00:20:18.408 --> 00:20:19.955
How about now?

00:20:19.955 --> 00:20:21.418
- [Martha] Yeah. (mumbles)

00:20:21.418 --> 00:20:24.001
- Okay, so they were just easy,

00:20:24.947 --> 00:20:26.656
you know, it was an easy decision,

00:20:26.656 --> 00:20:28.872
because they were there the whole time.

00:20:28.872 --> 00:20:31.119
And a lot of the reason
I started Empathy was

00:20:31.119 --> 00:20:33.522
because of John and Nate.

00:20:33.522 --> 00:20:37.351
So it wasn't difficult to
decide who the work with,

00:20:37.351 --> 00:20:39.804
it was right in front of my face.

00:20:39.804 --> 00:20:42.929
- [Martha] Yeah, I mean, I
think the wine. (mumbles)

00:20:42.929 --> 00:20:46.612
- Oh, I apologize, that
was very, that was fun.

00:20:46.612 --> 00:20:50.006
That was predicated on
tasting a lot of wine,

00:20:50.006 --> 00:20:53.456
and figuring out which vineyards
were sourcing those wines,

00:20:53.456 --> 00:20:56.133
and then going and meeting
with the vineyards,

00:20:56.133 --> 00:20:59.599
and just deciding who
I had chemistry with,

00:20:59.599 --> 00:21:01.666
and who I most enjoyed working with,

00:21:01.666 --> 00:21:05.166
and who really clicked with us personally.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:11.627
- [Martha] Okay, how did you. (mumbles)

00:21:15.179 --> 00:21:16.553
- You know, we negotiated directly

00:21:16.553 --> 00:21:18.978
with the vineyards, you
know, they're in the business

00:21:18.978 --> 00:21:20.930
of selling grapes, so it wasn't hard

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:23.055
to convince them to work with us,

00:21:23.055 --> 00:21:25.765
other than I wanted
more aggressive pricing

00:21:25.765 --> 00:21:28.004
because I wanted to pass
that onto the consumer,

00:21:28.004 --> 00:21:30.079
and position the brand
in the right price point.

00:21:30.079 --> 00:21:31.940
So, you know, there was probably eight

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:34.380
or nine vineyards that we got down to,

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:36.501
and then we started doing
business negotiations,

00:21:36.501 --> 00:21:39.158
and five to six emerged
of willing to do it

00:21:39.158 --> 00:21:40.830
in the price points that we wanted.

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:43.256
And then we let the
growing season play out.

00:21:43.256 --> 00:21:45.263
It was unfortunately a difficult year,

00:21:45.263 --> 00:21:46.699
because there was some big fires,

00:21:46.699 --> 00:21:48.472
so some of the people we were working

00:21:48.472 --> 00:21:51.201
with had smoke issues, so that's just kind

00:21:51.201 --> 00:21:54.618
of how we got down to our final partners.

00:21:55.711 --> 00:21:59.126
- [Martha] Okay. (mumbles)

00:21:59.126 --> 00:22:01.538
- No, no, it didn't delay the release,

00:22:01.538 --> 00:22:04.432
it just, it eliminated
one farmer, specifically,

00:22:04.432 --> 00:22:06.580
that I was pretty fond of, unfortunately,

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:09.734
they just had too much smoke damage.

00:22:09.734 --> 00:22:14.234
- [Martha] Great, okay, so. (mumbles)

00:22:16.355 --> 00:22:17.816
- Yep, that's the right word, you know,

00:22:17.816 --> 00:22:20.303
that's the right word,
we're making 30,000 cases,

00:22:20.303 --> 00:22:24.585
10,000 cases of each flavor,
red, white, and rosé.

00:22:24.585 --> 00:22:28.756
- [Martha] Okay, but you chose. (mumbles)

00:22:28.756 --> 00:22:32.529
- 'Cause it's ready the
soonest, so it's all

00:22:32.529 --> 00:22:35.347
from the same vintage, and the release

00:22:35.347 --> 00:22:39.372
of rosé, then white, then red
is completely predicated on,

00:22:39.372 --> 00:22:41.128
you know, how long it
has to stay in barrel,

00:22:41.128 --> 00:22:43.769
and which wines are ready the soonest,

00:22:43.769 --> 00:22:45.866
and things of that nature.

00:22:45.866 --> 00:22:50.866
- [Martha] Yeah, okay, and
what were you. (mumbles)

00:22:52.556 --> 00:22:54.267
- The biggest thing I was
paying attention to was,

00:22:54.267 --> 00:22:56.695
could we make it delicious, like,

00:22:56.695 --> 00:23:00.291
I think people over-complicate
wine in the business,

00:23:00.291 --> 00:23:02.129
they try to get too
nerdy, too complicated,

00:23:02.129 --> 00:23:05.065
too scientific, too artistic.

00:23:05.065 --> 00:23:08.060
I'm very basic, can I make
a wine that the person

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:10.359
that drinks it thinks
is downright delicious,

00:23:10.359 --> 00:23:12.859
and wants to drink more of it.

00:23:14.155 --> 00:23:18.905
- [Martha] Okay, and would it. (mumbles)

00:23:21.971 --> 00:23:24.091
- You know, that's funny you say that.

00:23:24.091 --> 00:23:26.350
There was thoughts I
had, that I want to use,

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:28.390
for example, for the rosé, I thought

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:31.495
I was gonna skew more towards
pinot noir and Grenache,

00:23:31.495 --> 00:23:34.238
we ended up using more Syrah and Cinsaut.

00:23:34.238 --> 00:23:36.530
So, you know, one of the
things I'm always trying

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:40.415
to be careful of is trying
to make decisions up front

00:23:40.415 --> 00:23:42.575
that don't play out in reality.

00:23:42.575 --> 00:23:44.870
So there's this series of
seven to 10 different grapes

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:47.235
that could have been used,
and then we just tasted

00:23:47.235 --> 00:23:50.658
and blended, and then
wherever delicious came out,

00:23:50.658 --> 00:23:52.480
we worked backwards, there was no emotion

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:55.881
to use more pinot noir and less, you know,

00:23:55.881 --> 00:23:57.443
cabernet, you know, it was just,

00:23:57.443 --> 00:24:01.200
what were the best
varietals, the best blend

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.533
of them to produce the style

00:24:04.543 --> 00:24:07.626
of wine that we were looking to make.

00:24:08.803 --> 00:24:10.263
- [Martha] Okay, and how involved were

00:24:10.263 --> 00:24:13.014
you in the process? (mumbles)

00:24:13.014 --> 00:24:14.982
- Oh, I basically made
the decisions, like,

00:24:14.982 --> 00:24:16.972
I didn't want to put anybody, like,

00:24:16.972 --> 00:24:18.912
I didn't want to put
anybody in a vulnerable spot

00:24:18.912 --> 00:24:21.189
in year one, if it wasn't gonna work,

00:24:21.189 --> 00:24:23.595
I didn't want to blame John,
or Nate, or anybody else.

00:24:23.595 --> 00:24:27.311
So I was, you know, it's
funny, we have the footage

00:24:27.311 --> 00:24:29.524
of the blending sessions.

00:24:29.524 --> 00:24:31.541
I think people would be
flabbergasted how much

00:24:31.541 --> 00:24:35.826
I was driving the boat, I have
a very distinct understanding

00:24:35.826 --> 00:24:40.826
of what the consumer wants
to drink, and I definitely,

00:24:41.111 --> 00:24:43.519
out of all the, out of all the things

00:24:43.519 --> 00:24:46.246
that have been involved with Empathy,

00:24:46.246 --> 00:24:48.547
the actual wine is probably the thing

00:24:48.547 --> 00:24:50.452
that I've been most aggressive about.

00:24:50.452 --> 00:24:51.819
Which makes me laugh because

00:24:51.819 --> 00:24:54.272
I think the outside
perception is probably,

00:24:54.272 --> 00:24:55.832
that's the thing I'm probably
least paying attention to,

00:24:55.832 --> 00:24:57.951
and the business, and the marketing.

00:24:57.951 --> 00:25:01.117
But the reality is, I
was very, very involved

00:25:01.117 --> 00:25:02.700
in the wine itself.

00:25:06.817 --> 00:25:10.400
- [Martha] Yeah. (mumbles)

00:25:12.568 --> 00:25:14.167
- Yes, (laughs) yes.

00:25:14.167 --> 00:25:15.333
(Martha mumbles)

00:25:15.333 --> 00:25:16.580
Yes, for sure.

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:21.580
- [Martha] Yes, well, what do
you want people to. (mumbles)

00:25:21.997 --> 00:25:24.096
- I want them to be
surprised how good it is,

00:25:24.096 --> 00:25:26.971
honestly, that is my, you know, look,

00:25:26.971 --> 00:25:28.709
when people drink wine,
they feel a lot of things,

00:25:28.709 --> 00:25:30.522
either excitement, sorrow, I mean,

00:25:30.522 --> 00:25:34.043
there's a lot of things
out of my control that,

00:25:34.043 --> 00:25:35.839
when people are drinking wine.

00:25:35.839 --> 00:25:38.964
What is in my control is
them being flabbergasted

00:25:38.964 --> 00:25:41.589
and surprised by the
quality, and we did set out

00:25:41.589 --> 00:25:44.714
to produce $40.00 to
$50.00 wine for $20.00.

00:25:44.714 --> 00:25:46.436
That is a very big goal of mine,

00:25:46.436 --> 00:25:49.579
and the feedback so far
has been remarkable,

00:25:49.579 --> 00:25:51.048
and I think it's because the wine's

00:25:51.048 --> 00:25:54.769
just been a lot better than
people thought it would be.

00:25:54.769 --> 00:25:59.204
- [Martha] Okay, how much
have you moved already?

00:25:59.204 --> 00:26:02.465
- I think the, I think maybe, let,

00:26:02.465 --> 00:26:04.543
I want to, let's make sure
we give you the right number

00:26:04.543 --> 00:26:05.750
because I don't want to
give it to you wrong,

00:26:05.750 --> 00:26:08.902
but I think we're about 9,000
cases in, at this point.

00:26:08.902 --> 00:26:10.601
I mean, the white and the
red aren't even out yet,

00:26:10.601 --> 00:26:12.764
so there's only so much we can do.

00:26:12.764 --> 00:26:13.764
- [Martha] How many did you say?

00:26:13.764 --> 00:26:14.597
- 9,000.

00:26:16.304 --> 00:26:17.137
- [Martha] 9,000?

00:26:17.137 --> 00:26:18.384
- Mm-hmm.

00:26:18.384 --> 00:26:21.134
(Martha mumbles)

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:33.665
So in the US, it's completely
100% direct-to-consumer,

00:26:33.665 --> 00:26:36.711
but internationally, the cost
would have been too expensive

00:26:36.711 --> 00:26:41.162
to ship individually, and since
I've known the Vinomofo guys

00:26:41.162 --> 00:26:45.225
for a long time, I just felt
like I was comfortable making

00:26:45.225 --> 00:26:49.475
that partnership, and we
took a pretty substantial,

00:26:50.744 --> 00:26:53.103
you know, hit financially because we had

00:26:53.103 --> 00:26:56.218
to still create the integrity of the price

00:26:56.218 --> 00:26:58.459
for the end consumer.

00:26:58.459 --> 00:27:01.022
So that's how we ended up
doing it in that scenario.

00:27:01.022 --> 00:27:04.034
So to your point, it's
obviously direct-to-consumer

00:27:04.034 --> 00:27:07.780
in the US, but it is not
direct-to-consumer internationally,

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:12.780
because we need a retail, or a
distribution kind of partner.

00:27:12.862 --> 00:27:14.471
- [Martha] Okay, just wanted to check.

00:27:14.471 --> 00:27:15.568
- Yeah.

00:27:15.568 --> 00:27:19.068
- [Martha] And. (mumbles)

00:27:22.406 --> 00:27:25.073
Yeah. (mumbles)

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:31.900
- For the wine?
(Martha mumbles)

00:27:34.385 --> 00:27:36.328
It's--
(Martha mumbles)

00:27:36.328 --> 00:27:38.543
It's 'cause Vinomofo had the capabilities,

00:27:38.543 --> 00:27:41.035
and because I have an office
here, and I'm here a lot,

00:27:41.035 --> 00:27:42.608
and so it's just a good market for me.

00:27:42.608 --> 00:27:46.661
And so it was exciting to
be able to offer it here.

00:27:46.661 --> 00:27:51.501
- [Martha] And when can
we expect the. (mumbles)

00:27:51.501 --> 00:27:53.427
