which didn't happen until
I was in my mid-30s,

00:57:31.389 --> 00:57:33.630
so I never even, like,
saw that as something

00:57:33.630 --> 00:57:34.903
I would ever do, I mean,

00:57:34.903 --> 00:57:39.072
I was running a wine
E-commerce and retail business.

00:57:39.072 --> 00:57:40.478
I remember my mom watched, like,

00:57:40.478 --> 00:57:41.637
one of my first speeches, she said, like,

00:57:41.637 --> 00:57:43.718
"What the fuck is that?"
(audience laughs)

00:57:43.718 --> 00:57:46.007
And I was like, "Yeah, I don't," so.

00:57:46.007 --> 00:57:47.580
- Do you stop cursing
when you drink your wine,

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:48.898
or how does it? (laughs)

00:57:48.898 --> 00:57:52.295
- No, I get sleepy when
I drink wine, so, no,

00:57:52.295 --> 00:57:56.344
I can give plenty of
advice without cursing.

00:57:56.344 --> 00:57:58.123
- Okay, that's cool, that's good to know.

00:57:58.123 --> 00:58:00.119
So having worked with
so many entrepreneurs,

00:58:00.119 --> 00:58:02.681
and maybe seen in your travels that,

00:58:02.681 --> 00:58:04.807
are there any, like,
similarities wherever you go,

00:58:04.807 --> 00:58:07.998
or differences, especially here in Asia?

00:58:07.998 --> 00:58:11.081
- Yeah, I mean, you know, it's funny.

00:58:12.950 --> 00:58:15.066
What I really recognize in Asia,

00:58:15.066 --> 00:58:18.134
and as everybody here knows,
especially if you're here,

00:58:18.134 --> 00:58:19.492
it's just like anything else.

00:58:19.492 --> 00:58:22.523
There's so many subcultures,
it's tough to bucket,

00:58:22.523 --> 00:58:25.076
especially, you know, if you
think about Southeast Asia,

00:58:25.076 --> 00:58:27.914
there's so many different,
using a wine term.

00:58:27.914 --> 00:58:29.188
(speaking in foreign language)

00:58:29.188 --> 00:58:32.960
There's nuances, but I will say that the

00:58:32.960 --> 00:58:34.432
kind of

00:58:34.432 --> 00:58:37.349
raw entrepreneurial spirit

00:58:37.349 --> 00:58:41.349
in Asia feels exciting
to me, you know, I think,

00:58:42.295 --> 00:58:43.378
I think that,

00:58:44.241 --> 00:58:47.574
you know, Europe doesn't have it at all.

00:58:48.545 --> 00:58:52.495
I think America's version of it right now

00:58:52.495 --> 00:58:54.912
is a little interesting,

00:58:54.912 --> 00:58:58.138
from the standpoint of,
there's just so many people

00:58:58.138 --> 00:59:01.247
that want to be entrepreneurs,
'cause it got cool,

00:59:01.247 --> 00:59:05.916
that we're living through
a really interesting time.

00:59:05.916 --> 00:59:07.275
But there's,

00:59:07.275 --> 00:59:11.128
there's a mix of anxiety and opportunity

00:59:11.128 --> 00:59:13.096
in the Asian market that
I think has a little bit

00:59:13.096 --> 00:59:16.481
of a raw entrepreneurial
spirit that is very attractive

00:59:16.481 --> 00:59:19.264
to me, like, I feel like there's,

00:59:19.264 --> 00:59:21.552
there's a lot of opportunity,

00:59:21.552 --> 00:59:25.270
and a lot of ambition,
and I think a lot of,

00:59:25.270 --> 00:59:26.552
you've got the first generation

00:59:26.552 --> 00:59:28.946
that's kind of breaking away
from what their parents want

00:59:28.946 --> 00:59:31.601
them to do at scale,
to do entrepreneurship,

00:59:31.601 --> 00:59:35.393
which has a really
intriguing dynamic to it.

00:59:35.393 --> 00:59:38.643
So the similarity is, at its most pure,

00:59:39.739 --> 00:59:43.510
I think entrepreneurs aren't scared,

00:59:43.510 --> 00:59:46.896
and that is the one core thing.

00:59:46.896 --> 00:59:48.745
And I think the reason I'm aware

00:59:48.745 --> 00:59:51.309
that we're living through an over-inflated

00:59:51.309 --> 00:59:54.529
entrepreneurial market is I
do think we have executives

00:59:54.529 --> 00:59:58.020
and students acting as
entrepreneurs in this era.

00:59:58.020 --> 01:00:02.197
And so there's more fear,
and more Excel sheets,

01:00:02.197 --> 01:00:05.375
and bullshit in this era, which is fine.

01:00:05.375 --> 01:00:06.959
I mean, I don't get to judge, but it

01:00:06.959 --> 01:00:10.246
definitely feels like we're in
a bubble of entrepreneurship.

01:00:10.246 --> 01:00:14.317
And that usually means it's
gonna burst at some point.

01:00:14.317 --> 01:00:15.970
- So what are your
thoughts, like, obviously,

01:00:15.970 --> 01:00:18.362
like, in Asia, we don't
really have that much

01:00:18.362 --> 01:00:22.326
of these developing kind
of types of VC ecosystem,

01:00:22.326 --> 01:00:23.974
and you talk to a lot of entrepreneurs

01:00:23.974 --> 01:00:26.618
in (mumbles), right, so any thoughts on--

01:00:26.618 --> 01:00:28.804
- I think that's the, I mean,
I'm glad you brought that up,

01:00:28.804 --> 01:00:30.084
and that's where I was gonna go next.

01:00:30.084 --> 01:00:32.970
I think the great, the great strength

01:00:32.970 --> 01:00:34.828
of a lot of the,

01:00:34.828 --> 01:00:36.970
the entrepreneurial ventures
that I've come across

01:00:36.970 --> 01:00:39.878
in this region is predicated on the fact

01:00:39.878 --> 01:00:42.167
that outside of a small group of people,

01:00:42.167 --> 01:00:44.678
there isn't as much funding,

01:00:44.678 --> 01:00:47.072
and I think funding has
really destroyed the merit

01:00:47.072 --> 01:00:49.666
of entrepreneurship in the US

01:00:49.666 --> 01:00:51.371
and parts of Asia, for that matter.

01:00:51.371 --> 01:00:54.623
And so, you know, it doesn't take a hero

01:00:54.623 --> 01:00:57.336
to lose money each month.

01:00:57.336 --> 01:00:59.976
And that's what we have right now.

01:00:59.976 --> 01:01:03.485
And so I, you're right, I do
encourage people learning how

01:01:03.485 --> 01:01:07.288
to make money, I think
it's a much better idea.

01:01:07.288 --> 01:01:08.644
You build something much more real,

01:01:08.644 --> 01:01:10.823
this notion of, just keep fundraising

01:01:10.823 --> 01:01:15.195
until an exit feels like
a game, not a business.

01:01:15.195 --> 01:01:16.812
- Cool, maybe

01:01:16.812 --> 01:01:19.809
we'll open this time to any
startups on the floor, so yeah.

01:01:19.809 --> 01:01:20.642
