underlining technology,

01:04:16.435 --> 01:04:18.094
I'm outrageously bullish on,

01:04:18.094 --> 01:04:23.094
no different than the internet
itself back in the day.

01:04:23.199 --> 01:04:25.839
The currencies that layer on top of it,

01:04:25.839 --> 01:04:28.222
I view very similar to the search engines

01:04:28.222 --> 01:04:30.906
and the browsers of the, you know,

01:04:30.906 --> 01:04:35.038
late '90s, early 2000s,
which is the same reason

01:04:35.038 --> 01:04:37.258
you were able to raise so much capital,

01:04:37.258 --> 01:04:39.875
and that it's a little
harder now to do that,

01:04:39.875 --> 01:04:41.403
though there is some resurgence.

01:04:41.403 --> 01:04:45.483
Like, ICOs were no different
than the 2001 internet,

01:04:45.483 --> 01:04:48.876
you know, blip, there was
completely irrational behavior,

01:04:48.876 --> 01:04:50.894
because there's a lot
of money in the system,

01:04:50.894 --> 01:04:52.749
and people were literally
walking around, saying,

01:04:52.749 --> 01:04:55.180
we're gonna tokenize oxygen,
and people were like,

01:04:55.180 --> 01:04:57.226
here's a billion, like, it was very fun

01:04:57.226 --> 01:04:58.771
to watch, 'cause I'm like, man, this is

01:04:58.771 --> 01:05:00.999
always gonna be the way
everything's gonna be.

01:05:00.999 --> 01:05:04.365
New technologies will
emerge, and this will happen.

01:05:04.365 --> 01:05:06.595
And I think your ambition, I'm sure,

01:05:06.595 --> 01:05:08.405
and anybody else that's in this space,

01:05:08.405 --> 01:05:12.843
is to end up being, you
know, a Netscape Navigator,

01:05:12.843 --> 01:05:14.093
or a Google, or

01:05:15.101 --> 01:05:16.972
whatever the winners are.

01:05:16.972 --> 01:05:20.156
And so I think there'll be an
enormous amount of money lost,

01:05:20.156 --> 01:05:21.932
and there'll be a couple places

01:05:21.932 --> 01:05:23.646
where there's an enormous
amount of money made

01:05:23.646 --> 01:05:25.455
on the back of the infrastructure.

01:05:25.455 --> 01:05:27.388
But I think much like the way I thought

01:05:27.388 --> 01:05:30.638
about the internet in 1996, 1999, 2002,

01:05:32.196 --> 01:05:34.337
I don't, I think all of us are now aware,

01:05:34.337 --> 01:05:36.485
and have heard this, no
matter how much knowledge

01:05:36.485 --> 01:05:38.656
or not knowledge we have around Bitcoin,

01:05:38.656 --> 01:05:42.980
or crypto, or Blockchain,
it's in the ethos.

01:05:42.980 --> 01:05:45.693
But it's grossly
underestimated by the masses

01:05:45.693 --> 01:05:48.161
of what it looks like 15 years from today.

01:05:48.161 --> 01:05:49.926
And that's where I spend a lot of my time,

01:05:49.926 --> 01:05:53.316
trying to be thoughtful of, like, who's,

01:05:53.316 --> 01:05:55.636
what are the shovels and the picks

01:05:55.636 --> 01:05:57.086
of that kind of infrastructure,

01:05:57.086 --> 01:05:58.857
which inevitably doesn't have the layer

01:05:58.857 --> 01:06:00.577
and tollbooths that the internet has,

01:06:00.577 --> 01:06:02.974
because of the framework
of the technology.

01:06:02.974 --> 01:06:06.168
And so that's kind of how I see it.

01:06:06.168 --> 01:06:07.706
- I like how you mentioned
shovels and picks.

01:06:07.706 --> 01:06:09.875
Is that one, I mean,
having gone through so many

01:06:09.875 --> 01:06:12.220
of these trends, is that something you use

01:06:12.220 --> 01:06:13.887
to kind of cut through the noise?

01:06:13.887 --> 01:06:15.372
Because with every trend, you know,

01:06:15.372 --> 01:06:16.624
people get overwhelmed, right?

01:06:16.624 --> 01:06:18.488
Like, with all these jargon in trends,

01:06:18.488 --> 01:06:20.671
like, what, how do you look through, like,

01:06:20.671 --> 01:06:22.662
you know, business itself, trends,

01:06:22.662 --> 01:06:24.068
and then pick the winners?

01:06:24.068 --> 01:06:27.053
- Yeah, I think I do, and I
think some of it was intuitive.

01:06:27.053 --> 01:06:29.124
You know, I was a super
under-educated kid,

01:06:29.124 --> 01:06:31.315
I was a really bad
student, so a lot of stuff,

01:06:31.315 --> 01:06:33.205
I've learned almost absolutely everything,

01:06:33.205 --> 01:06:35.521
actually, I've learned
everything by doing,

01:06:35.521 --> 01:06:37.294
and going through it.

01:06:37.294 --> 01:06:39.544
Yeah, I think the gold rush

01:06:40.394 --> 01:06:44.443
of the 1800s in the US
continues to play out.

01:06:44.443 --> 01:06:47.730
And so I do think about shovels and picks.

01:06:47.730 --> 01:06:50.094
I think a lot about
people building companies

01:06:50.094 --> 01:06:53.225
on top of other companies,
and then being vulnerable.

01:06:53.225 --> 01:06:56.112
I see so much over-valuation,
people building

01:06:56.112 --> 01:06:58.907
on top of other things where, you know,

01:06:58.907 --> 01:07:00.344
it's very obvious, you know, it's funny

01:07:00.344 --> 01:07:03.103
to see Twitter reopen its API, you know?

01:07:03.103 --> 01:07:05.484
I saw the headlines, I haven't
read the documentation,

01:07:05.484 --> 01:07:07.010
but I'm like, oh, boy, you know,

01:07:07.010 --> 01:07:09.824
this was a funny, you
know, play a couple years,

01:07:09.824 --> 01:07:12.127
you know, a decade ago, I guess now.

01:07:12.127 --> 01:07:13.522
Yeah, I,

01:07:13.522 --> 01:07:15.924
now that I'm getting, you know, I'm 43,

01:07:15.924 --> 01:07:17.566
so this is, like, maybe
a second or a third

01:07:17.566 --> 01:07:19.864
or a fourth turn, you just
see the same things over

01:07:19.864 --> 01:07:22.614
and over, for me, the
thing that I always do,

01:07:22.614 --> 01:07:23.913
and that has been tried and true,

01:07:23.913 --> 01:07:26.844
and that, if I could leave
anything for anybody in here

01:07:26.844 --> 01:07:28.646
that I do think would be valuable,

01:07:28.646 --> 01:07:31.313
I reverse engineer the audience.

01:07:32.486 --> 01:07:34.670
Like, to me, what works for my content,

01:07:34.670 --> 01:07:36.117
what worked for me in the wine store,

01:07:36.117 --> 01:07:37.914
what works for us at VaynerMedia,

01:07:37.914 --> 01:07:41.039
work works always is, do you understand

01:07:41.039 --> 01:07:44.276
who you're servicing, the end consumer?

01:07:44.276 --> 01:07:47.446
And does every decision, thought process,

01:07:47.446 --> 01:07:49.771
and maneuver benefit that person?

01:07:49.771 --> 01:07:53.067
The cynicism and the scrutiny undertone

01:07:53.067 --> 01:07:55.618
that I started with is, I believe 99%

01:07:55.618 --> 01:07:58.007
of startups pander to venture capitalists,

01:07:58.007 --> 01:08:00.950
not the customer of their product.

01:08:00.950 --> 01:08:05.072
So inevitably, they're
already in trouble, and so

01:08:05.072 --> 01:08:07.252
one thing that would
bode well for all of you,

01:08:07.252 --> 01:08:09.213
in life, as a human, and definitely

01:08:09.213 --> 01:08:12.197
in your businesses, is
who are you servicing,

01:08:12.197 --> 01:08:15.187
and are you just religious about that?

01:08:15.187 --> 01:08:17.516
Oftentimes, people are
fascinated by how little

01:08:17.516 --> 01:08:19.690
I know about the industry I'm in.

01:08:19.690 --> 01:08:22.074
It's because I spend no
time on my competition

01:08:22.074 --> 01:08:25.255
or anything else, 'cause
it's just 100% predicated

01:08:25.255 --> 01:08:29.759
on the current state of the
end consumer of that product.

01:08:29.759 --> 01:08:32.208
And I think that will really matter.

01:08:32.208 --> 01:08:33.041
- Yes.

01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:35.807
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:08:35.807 --> 01:08:37.228
So good to finally meet
you, I've been following

01:08:37.228 --> 01:08:38.855
you for four years, I'm
sort of nervous right now.

01:08:38.855 --> 01:08:40.087
- Don't worry, thank you.

01:08:40.087 --> 01:08:41.002
- [Audience Member] I'm
sure you get that a lot.

01:08:41.002 --> 01:08:42.047
- I'm flattered.

01:08:42.047 --> 01:08:43.541
- [Audience Member] I never
thought that would happen,

01:08:43.541 --> 01:08:44.710
but yeah.
- Thank you.

01:08:44.710 --> 01:08:45.953
- [Audience Member] So,
I mean, like, I know

01:08:45.953 --> 01:08:48.296
you get this a lot, but you
fundamentally changed my life,

01:08:48.296 --> 01:08:52.463
man, 2017, quit my data
management consulting job.

01:08:53.983 --> 01:08:56.100
I come from India, I've lived in Singapore

01:08:56.100 --> 01:08:59.925
for about nine years,
got my cost of living,

01:08:59.925 --> 01:09:01.815
that was your call.

01:09:01.815 --> 01:09:05.784
Started a podcast for decrypt
Asia, it's a crypto podcast,

01:09:05.784 --> 01:09:09.611
the only one covering
crypto news from Asia.

01:09:09.611 --> 01:09:11.032
Started writing for tech in Asia,

01:09:11.032 --> 01:09:13.974
which is the equivalent of tech (mumbles).

01:09:13.974 --> 01:09:17.992
Started getting hit by
family offices, VCs,

01:09:17.992 --> 01:09:21.005
to figure out what I was up to, managed,

01:09:21.005 --> 01:09:24.032
so one of the VCs that hit me up,

01:09:24.032 --> 01:09:27.820
you know, it was a casual meeting from,

01:09:27.820 --> 01:09:31.099
from one of the articles
that I wrote on tech in Asia.

01:09:31.099 --> 01:09:34.030
They liked my views, so it
was a combination, I think

01:09:34.030 --> 01:09:36.603
you're on one extreme, and
then Naval Ravikant is kind

01:09:36.603 --> 01:09:39.484
of on the other extreme, and I
think my sort of philosophies

01:09:39.484 --> 01:09:41.363
of, like, fall somewhere in the middle

01:09:41.363 --> 01:09:46.259
of what you talk about,
and what Naval talks about.

01:09:46.259 --> 01:09:48.184
So he basically talks
about leveraging capital,

01:09:48.184 --> 01:09:53.184
media, and tech, and so the
podcast was leverage media.

01:09:53.238 --> 01:09:56.373
Managed to be the first
employee at a VC fund,

01:09:56.373 --> 01:09:59.985
crypto VC fund, built it up from scratch.

01:09:59.985 --> 01:10:02.589
So it's been about a year at this VC fund,

01:10:02.589 --> 01:10:05.708
but it's been an absolute, I mean,

01:10:05.708 --> 01:10:07.911
it's been the craziest
year of my life, man,

01:10:07.911 --> 01:10:09.052
so thanks a lot.
- It makes me happy.

01:10:09.052 --> 01:10:09.885
- [Audience Member] There's no question.

01:10:09.885 --> 01:10:10.718
(audience laughs)

01:10:10.718 --> 01:10:11.976
- Thank you. (laughs)
(audience laughs)

01:10:11.976 --> 01:10:13.488
(audience applauds)

01:10:13.488 --> 01:10:15.112
(overlapping chattering)

01:10:15.112 --> 01:10:15.945
- Awesome.

01:10:17.669 --> 01:10:19.434
I'm sure you get that
quite a lot on the street.

01:10:19.434 --> 01:10:21.968
- Yeah, I mean, look, I
think, I think, you know,

01:10:21.968 --> 01:10:24.905
it's a really fun segue from
what I just said earlier.

01:10:24.905 --> 01:10:27.911
Like, one of the reasons I have the life

01:10:27.911 --> 01:10:30.807
that I have is, every single
piece of content I put out

01:10:30.807 --> 01:10:33.199
in the internet, I
desperately, desperately,

01:10:33.199 --> 01:10:36.030
desperately am thinking subconsciously

01:10:36.030 --> 01:10:38.934
and consciously of, how
will this help somebody

01:10:38.934 --> 01:10:42.531
that consumes it, not, how
is this going to get likes

01:10:42.531 --> 01:10:45.455
or followers, so then I
can do a branded post,

01:10:45.455 --> 01:10:48.885
and make, like, when
you're, when you are 100%,

01:10:48.885 --> 01:10:52.082
not 99, and definitely not 30,

01:10:52.082 --> 01:10:55.909
when you are 100%
consumer-centric, audience-centric,

01:10:55.909 --> 01:10:58.491
incredibly good things happen.

01:10:58.491 --> 01:11:01.138
And I just, I'm fascinated
by people's inability

01:11:01.138 --> 01:11:04.137
to understand that providing
more value than you ask

01:11:04.137 --> 01:11:08.081
for in return is always
a tremendous model,

01:11:08.081 --> 01:11:12.563
and yet, people are so incapable
of playing the long game.

01:11:12.563 --> 01:11:14.842
People are so disproportionately insecure

01:11:14.842 --> 01:11:17.183
to prove they're successful to everybody,

01:11:17.183 --> 01:11:19.516
that they're pandering to such short-term.

01:11:19.516 --> 01:11:21.920
And so I believe the world
is filled with nothing

01:11:21.920 --> 01:11:24.734
but sprinters, and I'm a marathon runner,

01:11:24.734 --> 01:11:26.401
and life is actually a marathon.

01:11:26.401 --> 01:11:29.545
And so you can imagine the
confidence I roll through

01:11:29.545 --> 01:11:31.844
in the framework that I've created,

01:11:31.844 --> 01:11:35.302
and that comes from the
humility of being an immigrant,

01:11:35.302 --> 01:11:37.632
that comes from incredible fortune

01:11:37.632 --> 01:11:40.075
of having a mother who
disproportionately built self-esteem

01:11:40.075 --> 01:11:42.303
in me, which allows me not to pander

01:11:42.303 --> 01:11:46.476
to outside voices that lack
the context of who I am.

01:11:46.476 --> 01:11:49.420
And it's been very, it's
been very rewarding,

01:11:49.420 --> 01:11:51.535
and then what ends up happening is,

01:11:51.535 --> 01:11:52.947
when you start getting a little older,

01:11:52.947 --> 01:11:54.395
and you start becoming so grateful

01:11:54.395 --> 01:11:57.539
for the framework, 'cause you
realize you're the byproduct

01:11:57.539 --> 01:12:00.055
of circumstance and great parenting,

01:12:00.055 --> 01:12:01.865
it makes you then want to do things,

01:12:01.865 --> 01:12:05.137
like you're almost, I feel
like I'm almost guilted,

01:12:05.137 --> 01:12:07.919
and, you know, I always think
about guilt and gratitude.

01:12:07.919 --> 01:12:09.602
You know, I just,

01:12:09.602 --> 01:12:12.641
I feel like, you know those
flies go into the light

01:12:12.641 --> 01:12:15.081
and get buzzed and die, like,
they know what's happening,

01:12:15.081 --> 01:12:17.475
but they can't stop,
it's kind of how I feel

01:12:17.475 --> 01:12:19.137
about making content.

01:12:19.137 --> 01:12:20.287
You know, I'll be very frank with you,

01:12:20.287 --> 01:12:21.704
it was interesting to hear what you,

01:12:21.704 --> 01:12:23.494
your observation of
somebody who's consumed me.

01:12:23.494 --> 01:12:28.340
I think I am remarkably
underrated for my operational

01:12:28.340 --> 01:12:30.419
and business capabilities because

01:12:30.419 --> 01:12:33.332
of my over-exaggerated
personality in producing content,

01:12:33.332 --> 01:12:34.595
I'm being serious.

01:12:34.595 --> 01:12:37.566
You know, I've taken
VaynerMedia, as the CEO

01:12:37.566 --> 01:12:42.025
and COO, for that matter, I
have operated it every day,

01:12:42.025 --> 01:12:43.958
I've taken it from zero
to 200 million dollars

01:12:43.958 --> 01:12:46.034
in revenue with no cash infusion,

01:12:46.034 --> 01:12:47.800
started it in a conference room,

01:12:47.800 --> 01:12:49.576
it's now my second
company that I've built,

01:12:49.576 --> 01:12:52.007
early investor in
Facebook, Twitter, Tumblr,

01:12:52.007 --> 01:12:55.969
like, all that stuff, yet
it's, you know, I think,

01:12:55.969 --> 01:12:58.100
it's really fascinating
for me that the thing

01:12:58.100 --> 01:13:01.675
that I feel that I'm giving
the most with is the thing

01:13:01.675 --> 01:13:04.244
that also, in return,

01:13:04.244 --> 01:13:05.883
creates

01:13:05.883 --> 01:13:07.551
a narrative that

01:13:07.551 --> 01:13:09.181
actually, it doesn't bother me,

01:13:09.181 --> 01:13:11.668
but as you can imagine,
when you love what you do,

01:13:11.668 --> 01:13:13.246
and you think you're good at it,

01:13:13.246 --> 01:13:15.077
there's a fascinating relationship I have

01:13:15.077 --> 01:13:17.972
with my content, because
I absolutely am very aware

01:13:17.972 --> 01:13:21.098
that it undermines my
capabilities as an operator

01:13:21.098 --> 01:13:23.454
and an executive, which is fine,

01:13:23.454 --> 01:13:26.107
because that's so much
more fun to hear than,

01:13:26.107 --> 01:13:30.878
you know, than anything
else, but it is fascinating.

01:13:30.878 --> 01:13:32.784
- So you mentioned that
this is a marathon,

01:13:32.784 --> 01:13:36.974
and you had Wine Library,
and now VaynerMedia,

01:13:36.974 --> 01:13:38.482
at what, how,

01:13:38.482 --> 01:13:40.898
how many areas get you, like, in between?

01:13:40.898 --> 01:13:41.999
I mean, obviously people say, oh,

01:13:41.999 --> 01:13:44.754
we're successful and what you
do is you just make content,

01:13:44.754 --> 01:13:47.035
right, but I'm pretty sure
that a lot of happened,

01:13:47.035 --> 01:13:48.470
else, in between, so.

01:13:48.470 --> 01:13:50.614
- You know, I haven't had a macro failure,

01:13:50.614 --> 01:13:52.155
because I'm too much of an immigrant,

01:13:52.155 --> 01:13:55.019
and I don't over-leverage myself, right?

01:13:55.019 --> 01:13:58.502
So I haven't had a macro failure.

01:13:58.502 --> 01:14:01.672
I have micro failures on a daily basis.

01:14:01.672 --> 01:14:05.436
You know, I lose money all
the time on initiatives

01:14:05.436 --> 01:14:09.072
and executions and divisions and concepts,

01:14:09.072 --> 01:14:11.213
but I've never put myself out of business.

01:14:11.213 --> 01:14:13.681
And for me, it's just binary,
either you're making payroll,

01:14:13.681 --> 01:14:15.758
or you're not, and, you know,

01:14:15.758 --> 01:14:18.211
I've made payroll every single week

01:14:18.211 --> 01:14:20.653
since I was 22 years
old, and I proud of that,

01:14:20.653 --> 01:14:23.788
'cause it's practical as fuck.

01:14:23.788 --> 01:14:26.213
So, but yeah, I mean, you know,

01:14:26.213 --> 01:14:27.535
any time you're running businesses,

01:14:27.535 --> 01:14:30.782
you're making super duper
mistakes on an every day basis.

01:14:30.782 --> 01:14:32.069
And I think a lot of the mistakes

01:14:32.069 --> 01:14:35.221
I make are actually the things I don't do,

01:14:35.221 --> 01:14:36.719
versus the things that I do.

01:14:36.719 --> 01:14:38.198
When you're, when you get into a place

01:14:38.198 --> 01:14:40.076
where you're crippled by opportunity,

01:14:40.076 --> 01:14:42.895
you're just passing on
things left and right.

01:14:42.895 --> 01:14:46.436
And so, but, you know, the other thing,

01:14:46.436 --> 01:14:48.662
you know, it's funny where my
mind goes on this question,

01:14:48.662 --> 01:14:53.254
you know, I also don't
have a year to really dwell

01:14:53.254 --> 01:14:56.452
on losses, you know,
I always struggled to,

01:14:56.452 --> 01:14:58.662
like, come up with them,
because I feel like I give them

01:14:58.662 --> 01:15:00.481
so little value.

01:15:00.481 --> 01:15:04.381
Like, I'm just, you know, one
thing I wish more people did

01:15:04.381 --> 01:15:07.963
and had is the year to not dwell.

01:15:07.963 --> 01:15:11.727
Like, to me, I'm just
thrilled for the at bat,

01:15:11.727 --> 01:15:15.422
so it makes me hard to be upset about,

01:15:15.422 --> 01:15:17.831
you know, people just beat themselves up.

01:15:17.831 --> 01:15:20.460
And, like, I don't know, you know?

01:15:20.460 --> 01:15:22.320
- I do that every day.
- Do you?

01:15:22.320 --> 01:15:23.153
- Like, sometimes.

01:15:23.153 --> 01:15:26.716
- Yeah, I'm the other way,
I'm not delusional, right?

01:15:26.716 --> 01:15:29.260
It's not like, but I'm just,

01:15:29.260 --> 01:15:32.312
I just feel like it's
the cost of the game.

01:15:32.312 --> 01:15:35.078
Like, it's, you know, if
you're truly the leader

01:15:35.078 --> 01:15:38.032
of something, or if you're
truly, like, own something,

01:15:38.032 --> 01:15:41.461
or the last line of defense, by nature,

01:15:41.461 --> 01:15:43.583
everything is a problem, like,

01:15:43.583 --> 01:15:46.583
my entire day is 98% headaches.

01:15:47.946 --> 01:15:50.422
Like, I think an entrepreneur
is the closest thing

01:15:50.422 --> 01:15:52.522
to a firefighter, right, like, you're

01:15:52.522 --> 01:15:56.203
just on-call at all times for some shit.

01:15:56.203 --> 01:15:58.692
There is no relaxation, like, you know,

01:15:58.692 --> 01:16:01.035
you're just always on,
I don't like even being

01:16:01.035 --> 01:16:03.098
on a plane for 17 hours without wifi,

01:16:03.098 --> 01:16:05.676
because something's
inevitably gonna be a problem

01:16:05.676 --> 01:16:09.916
when you have 1,000 employees,
global this and that, so.

01:16:09.916 --> 01:16:13.333
I don't even think there's time to dwell.

01:16:14.706 --> 01:16:16.534
- [Brian] Hello, Gary, my name is Brian.

01:16:16.534 --> 01:16:18.614
So I'm just really passionate
about digital marketing.

01:16:18.614 --> 01:16:20.517
The truth is people keep beating you up--

01:16:20.517 --> 01:16:21.350
- That's a great question.

01:16:21.350 --> 01:16:23.395
- [Brian] How did you do it?

01:16:23.395 --> 01:16:27.502
- The first 10 or 20
people were either friends

01:16:27.502 --> 01:16:29.419
of my brother and I, or

01:16:30.644 --> 01:16:33.463
disproportionately delusional fans of me.

01:16:33.463 --> 01:16:34.728
(audience laughs)

01:16:34.728 --> 01:16:36.378
Because,

01:16:36.378 --> 01:16:40.211
because I wanted a very
insular family dynamic

01:16:41.732 --> 01:16:44.911
that was completely predicated on trust,

01:16:44.911 --> 01:16:47.208
so that we could go fast.

01:16:47.208 --> 01:16:51.757
I think speed is a stunningly
underrated, people talk

01:16:51.757 --> 01:16:56.547
about it, but I think people
prefer process over speed,

01:16:56.547 --> 01:16:58.141
it makes people comfortable.

01:16:58.141 --> 01:17:01.094
And I think speed is
unbelievably important,

01:17:01.094 --> 01:17:02.463
and the only way you get fast

01:17:02.463 --> 01:17:05.027
with people is when there's trust.

01:17:05.027 --> 01:17:07.338
The reason companies suck
is it's all politics,

01:17:07.338 --> 01:17:09.359
and everybody gets slow,
everybody's worried

01:17:09.359 --> 01:17:11.587
about shit that they
shouldn't be worried about,

01:17:11.587 --> 01:17:13.664
thus not the task at hand.

01:17:13.664 --> 01:17:15.997
So I would highly recommend,

01:17:17.422 --> 01:17:19.284
if you're capable, I had the advantage

01:17:19.284 --> 01:17:21.945
of being in my second
chapter of my career,

01:17:21.945 --> 01:17:23.610
and I also had the great luxury

01:17:23.610 --> 01:17:27.033
that my brother was a much
better student than I was,

01:17:27.033 --> 01:17:29.768
and hung out with much more
capable people than I did,

01:17:29.768 --> 01:17:33.312
and so that first group was easier.

01:17:33.312 --> 01:17:36.130
But I really believe the way I,

01:17:36.130 --> 01:17:39.412
listen, everybody needs to
run a company their way,

01:17:39.412 --> 01:17:41.895
because there's no right way,
there's only right for you.

01:17:41.895 --> 01:17:45.357
For me, every, the two
companies that I've really run,

01:17:45.357 --> 01:17:47.052
and, like, the things that I've impacted,

01:17:47.052 --> 01:17:50.228
we just had a very major exit
from an incubated company

01:17:50.228 --> 01:17:52.958
from my fund, Resy, which
just sold to American Express.

01:17:52.958 --> 01:17:55.545
And that one, as well,
like, it's the same model,

01:17:55.545 --> 01:17:58.525
which is, I treat businesses like I think

01:17:58.525 --> 01:18:01.368
about parenting children,
I believe the first three

01:18:01.368 --> 01:18:06.295
to five years is
disproportionate, obnoxious love,

01:18:06.295 --> 01:18:09.733
honey, warmth, like, nobody's scared,

01:18:09.733 --> 01:18:12.965
like, I take on all responsibility.

01:18:12.965 --> 01:18:15.347
And then I think about kind of, like,

01:18:15.347 --> 01:18:18.569
year seven through 12 as, like, okay,

01:18:18.569 --> 01:18:21.169
now we start eliminating entitlement.

01:18:21.169 --> 01:18:24.484
And I'll tell you, I think I
missed the mark at VaynerMedia,

01:18:24.484 --> 01:18:27.120
I think I started creating a lack

01:18:27.120 --> 01:18:30.229
of entitlement 18 to 12 months too late,

01:18:30.229 --> 01:18:31.767
which created a little
bit of a vulnerability

01:18:31.767 --> 01:18:34.066
for us for a period, and it was painful.

01:18:34.066 --> 01:18:35.736
You know, once, you know, a spoiled,

01:18:35.736 --> 01:18:39.161
rich kid getting off a drug is
tough, so we had a little bit

01:18:39.161 --> 01:18:41.845
of a tough year, because there
was too much entitlement.

01:18:41.845 --> 01:18:45.417
But I think anything you
can do to create safety

01:18:45.417 --> 01:18:48.611
and speed, I think those
two assets really matter

01:18:48.611 --> 01:18:50.576
in the first five years of a company.

01:18:50.576 --> 01:18:51.486
- [Brian] Okay, (mumbles), thank you.

01:18:51.486 --> 01:18:52.659
- You're welcome.

01:18:52.659 --> 01:18:54.504
- We got time for one more, I think.

01:18:54.504 --> 01:18:56.196
- [Audience Member] I recently
was watching an interview,

01:18:56.196 --> 01:18:58.503
which you declared that
you missed out on Uber.

01:18:58.503 --> 01:18:59.644
- Yes.
(audience member mumbles)

01:18:59.644 --> 01:19:00.957
- [Audience Member] Even
if Travis Kalanick pitch

01:19:00.957 --> 01:19:02.986
to you a couple of times--
- Two times.

01:19:02.986 --> 01:19:05.620
- [Audience Member] I think,
I think you read, the book,

01:19:05.620 --> 01:19:06.986
you wrote somewhere--

01:19:06.986 --> 01:19:09.288
- The first book I wrote, the
only person I thanked was him,

01:19:09.288 --> 01:19:10.765
besides my family.

01:19:10.765 --> 01:19:12.061
- [Audience Member] Do you have any advice

01:19:12.061 --> 01:19:14.150
on the most common errors you should avoid

01:19:14.150 --> 01:19:16.423
when you (mumbles) investing (mumbles)?

01:19:16.423 --> 01:19:18.090
- I think, you know,

01:19:19.245 --> 01:19:21.659
you know what's funny, the
mistake I made there was

01:19:21.659 --> 01:19:26.648
I was too logical, right,
like, it was a side project,

01:19:26.648 --> 01:19:29.510
and I don't think side
projects tend to turn,

01:19:29.510 --> 01:19:32.522
especially 'cause I knew
Garrett and Travis super well,

01:19:32.522 --> 01:19:34.090
and they were angel investing,
and doing other stuff,

01:19:34.090 --> 01:19:36.665
and I'm like, they're distracted.

01:19:36.665 --> 01:19:38.029
So,

01:19:38.029 --> 01:19:42.608
you know, God, the, it is
such an imperfect science,

01:19:42.608 --> 01:19:44.275
investing, you know,

01:19:45.308 --> 01:19:47.408
here are a couple things that have really,

01:19:47.408 --> 01:19:50.260
here's where I'm going with the
rest of my investing career.

01:19:50.260 --> 01:19:52.075
I'm only gonna invest on two core things

01:19:52.075 --> 01:19:54.489
that happen with me through
my pattern of recognition.

01:19:54.489 --> 01:19:57.518
One, I intuitively believe in it, blindly,

01:19:57.518 --> 01:19:59.943
no logic against it, I have the feeling,

01:19:59.943 --> 01:20:01.939
I have the goosebumps, the
hair on the back of my head,

01:20:01.939 --> 01:20:05.429
I feel it, it feels it, I'm going, two,

01:20:05.429 --> 01:20:07.104
I'm now, at this point in my career,

01:20:07.104 --> 01:20:09.470
a believer that it's 80% the founder,

01:20:09.470 --> 01:20:13.296
