- [Maha] And you're very
patient, I want to do so much,

01:50:14.399 --> 01:50:16.347
and I'm just like, okay, just.

01:50:16.347 --> 01:50:17.180
- We will.

01:50:17.180 --> 01:50:18.250
- [Maha] Slow and steady, and just--

01:50:18.250 --> 01:50:19.308
- We will.

01:50:19.308 --> 01:50:21.438
- [Maha] And the fact that
I know that you aren't,

01:50:21.438 --> 01:50:23.545
like, you know, ramming it down, like,

01:50:23.545 --> 01:50:25.735
what's the one, like, that you are saying,

01:50:25.735 --> 01:50:28.587
slow and steady makes me comfortable,

01:50:28.587 --> 01:50:31.396
that I can now grow it in a way that's--

01:50:31.396 --> 01:50:32.542
- Yeah, I mean, it's--

01:50:32.542 --> 01:50:34.213
- [Maha] If you were putting
pressure on me, that would--

01:50:34.213 --> 01:50:35.376
- Where's my article?

01:50:35.376 --> 01:50:39.259
- [Maha] Yeah, that would
make me feel worse about--

01:50:39.259 --> 01:50:43.259
- I only put pressure on
DRock, other than that.

01:50:44.430 --> 01:50:47.014
He gets so much gold,
and just is incapable

01:50:47.014 --> 01:50:48.814
of getting it into the marketplace.

01:50:48.814 --> 01:50:51.615
- [Maha] When I know, like,
if you were to say to me,

01:50:51.615 --> 01:50:54.824
do three things, then I
would just want to do 10,

01:50:54.824 --> 01:50:55.972
you know what I mean?
- I'm sure.

01:50:55.972 --> 01:50:58.463
- [DRock] I'm holding the camera.

01:50:58.463 --> 01:51:00.630
- My best thing right now.

01:51:02.049 --> 01:51:04.873
What was the thing that,
there was such a fun one.

01:51:04.873 --> 01:51:06.289
Oh, God, it's gonna be hard to remember.

01:51:06.289 --> 01:51:09.980
It was, like, two months
ago, like, a month ago?

01:51:09.980 --> 01:51:14.886
Have I ever fallen, have I
ever fallen asleep on stage?

01:51:14.886 --> 01:51:15.969
- [DRock] No.

01:51:17.325 --> 01:51:19.006
- I'm super tired.

01:51:19.006 --> 01:51:19.839
- [Maha] You are?

01:51:19.839 --> 01:51:20.903
- Yeah, I haven't, I haven't slept

01:51:20.903 --> 01:51:23.454
for 20 fucking four hours.

01:51:23.454 --> 01:51:26.519
- [Maha] Is this our car when we finish?

01:51:26.519 --> 01:51:28.204
- [DRock] Right, you're taking, you're--

01:51:28.204 --> 01:51:29.399
- [Assistant] Answer to the question

01:51:29.399 --> 01:51:33.009
for the kid about
growing the team was be--

01:51:33.009 --> 01:51:36.176
- Mm-hmm, macro patience, micro speed.

01:51:37.802 --> 01:51:38.975
- [Maha] Hey, you gotta--

01:51:38.975 --> 01:51:43.105
- Operationally, operationally
you gotta go fast,

01:51:43.105 --> 01:51:46.459
you know, theoretically,
like, what you're up to,

01:51:46.459 --> 01:51:47.292
it makes sense.

01:51:47.292 --> 01:51:48.125
- [Maha] That's so cool.

01:51:48.125 --> 01:51:50.235
- That's why I'm a hard read, I'm a very,

01:51:50.235 --> 01:51:52.974
very hard read, 'cause, like,

01:51:52.974 --> 01:51:56.070
it is pulling from opposite directions.

01:51:56.070 --> 01:51:58.349
It looks like it's a contradiction,

01:51:58.349 --> 01:52:01.253
but if you think about it visually,

01:52:01.253 --> 01:52:04.370
I'm telling you up here to go slow,

01:52:04.370 --> 01:52:06.512
I'm telling you down here to go fast.

01:52:06.512 --> 01:52:08.980
So people think it's all one plane,

01:52:08.980 --> 01:52:11.007
all my content, all my
vision, everything I am,

01:52:11.007 --> 01:52:12.314
they think it's one plane, so, like,

01:52:12.314 --> 01:52:13.845
what the fuck, it doesn't make sense.

01:52:13.845 --> 01:52:16.401
What they don't understand is it's this.

01:52:16.401 --> 01:52:21.132
In real life, in the most
important shit, it's slow.

01:52:21.132 --> 01:52:23.643
Down here, all this shit means nothing,

01:52:23.643 --> 01:52:25.902
you have to go fast, but
people go slow down here.

01:52:25.902 --> 01:52:27.581
Have to be thoughtful,
let's have meanings,

01:52:27.581 --> 01:52:30.342
let's fucking over-process
it, but up here,

01:52:30.342 --> 01:52:31.637
they're like, money now, money now.

01:52:31.637 --> 01:52:34.372
Like, everyone's got it reversed.

01:52:34.372 --> 01:52:35.455
Got it?
- Yup.

01:52:37.299 --> 01:52:39.169
- Good job catching that, DRock.

01:52:39.169 --> 01:52:42.048
- [DRock] Almost put my camera down.

01:52:42.048 --> 01:52:43.826
- But that was a good visual to it,

01:52:43.826 --> 01:52:45.809
that was a good way to
explain it, that's what it is.

01:52:45.809 --> 01:52:48.226
(slow music)

01:53:00.069 --> 01:53:01.798
- All right, why Singapore, actually?

01:53:01.798 --> 01:53:04.588
I heard the initial plan
was to go into China first,

01:53:04.588 --> 01:53:07.373
and then to Asia, but,
you know, why the change?

01:53:07.373 --> 01:53:09.159
- I don't, I don't think
that's necessarily true.

01:53:09.159 --> 01:53:11.647
You know, I think, you
know, I'm not sure where

01:53:11.647 --> 01:53:13.203
that comes from, or, like, if I've--

01:53:13.203 --> 01:53:14.036
(interviewer mumbles)

01:53:14.036 --> 01:53:17.589
Or maybe, you know, to me, it's, yeah, no.

01:53:17.589 --> 01:53:19.050
There was never the initial plan,

01:53:19.050 --> 01:53:23.249
to me, Mainland China has
always been a far away thing,

01:53:23.249 --> 01:53:25.216
because I know I want to do, you know,

01:53:25.216 --> 01:53:26.719
kind of a partnership into that market,

01:53:26.719 --> 01:53:28.487
and it'll take time to build
that kind of relationship

01:53:28.487 --> 01:53:30.861
that would make me feel comfortable.

01:53:30.861 --> 01:53:32.881
But Southeast Asia has been kind of

01:53:32.881 --> 01:53:37.117
on the roadmap for at least
three years at this point.

01:53:37.117 --> 01:53:40.872
Just, it's extremely obvious
that the growth is here,

01:53:40.872 --> 01:53:43.021
and more importantly, 10 years ago,

01:53:43.021 --> 01:53:46.593
I set out to build a global
communications structure,

01:53:46.593 --> 01:53:48.619
and so it was just inevitable.

01:53:48.619 --> 01:53:50.899
And I think some of it is circumstance,

01:53:50.899 --> 01:53:53.149
you know, just serendipity,

01:53:54.396 --> 01:53:57.229
but obviously it's a very, I mean,

01:53:58.521 --> 01:54:00.226
this is maybe my fourth
or fifth trip here.

01:54:00.226 --> 01:54:01.559
And so there's a comfort for me here,

01:54:01.559 --> 01:54:03.637
there's comfort to do business here.

01:54:03.637 --> 01:54:07.733
My CFO in the Publicis
holding company spent five

01:54:07.733 --> 01:54:11.213
to seven years out here, so
there's just the familiarity,

01:54:11.213 --> 01:54:15.293
and a lot of our global
Fortune 500 clients have

01:54:15.293 --> 01:54:18.667
their offices here, and so
it just became very obvious.

01:54:18.667 --> 01:54:20.350
- Are there plans to also support some

01:54:20.350 --> 01:54:22.043
of the Singapore listed companies

01:54:22.043 --> 01:54:24.772
that (mumbles) as well listed
on the Singapore Exchange?

01:54:24.772 --> 01:54:27.162
- 100%, I mean, we're,

01:54:27.162 --> 01:54:28.468
we're coming here to do business

01:54:28.468 --> 01:54:30.076
with every single person that's interested

01:54:30.076 --> 01:54:31.804
in doing business with us, so.

01:54:31.804 --> 01:54:33.264
- So you're focused on what kind

01:54:33.264 --> 01:54:34.886
of sectors you want to target?

01:54:34.886 --> 01:54:38.844
- No, anybody who wants to
communicate to end consumers

01:54:38.844 --> 01:54:43.700
in a B-to-B or B-to-C
environment, and recognizes

01:54:43.700 --> 01:54:46.140
that the capability of how to do that

01:54:46.140 --> 01:54:49.101
in the actual world we live in in 2019

01:54:49.101 --> 01:54:53.643
and 2020 is our interest,
and that means everybody.

01:54:53.643 --> 01:54:54.924
- You mentioned Singapore

01:54:54.924 --> 01:54:59.026
and Southeast Asia have huge
potential, how much revenue do

01:54:59.026 --> 01:55:03.776
you think that's gonna generate
for you, any projections?

01:55:05.443 --> 01:55:06.876
- No.

01:55:06.876 --> 01:55:10.064
But to try to bring new value, like,

01:55:10.064 --> 01:55:12.666
from an ambition standpoint, you know,

01:55:12.666 --> 01:55:15.589
we'd like to be doing, you know,

01:55:15.589 --> 01:55:17.752
tens of millions of dollars in revenue

01:55:17.752 --> 01:55:20.691
in the next 36 to 48 months,
'cause we think the potential

01:55:20.691 --> 01:55:23.940
in the marketplace is here, so.

01:55:23.940 --> 01:55:26.627
You know, because I'm
building it for myself,

01:55:26.627 --> 01:55:27.853
you know, I think what's unusual

01:55:27.853 --> 01:55:30.980
about VaynerMedia is, I'm not trying to,

01:55:30.980 --> 01:55:34.057
you know, maximize profit
because I'm publicly traded.

01:55:34.057 --> 01:55:36.603
I'm not trying to flip
it to a holding company,

01:55:36.603 --> 01:55:38.624
so I'm not trying to maximize by EBITDA.

01:55:38.624 --> 01:55:41.137
I'm trying to build
something that's evergreen

01:55:41.137 --> 01:55:42.423
and in perpetuity, that I can use

01:55:42.423 --> 01:55:44.642
for my future business behaviors.

01:55:44.642 --> 01:55:47.217
So, as you can imagine, the short-term

01:55:47.217 --> 01:55:49.891
or medium-term revenue
goals is not something

01:55:49.891 --> 01:55:51.967
I'm spending any time to calculate.

01:55:51.967 --> 01:55:55.533
It's, get established,
understand the marketplace,

01:55:55.533 --> 01:55:59.992
understand the opportunities,
whether it's in the nuances

01:55:59.992 --> 01:56:04.801
of American-centric digital
companies, or if it's Asian,

01:56:04.801 --> 01:56:07.520
you know, whether it's
Line, or Cow, or, you know,

01:56:07.520 --> 01:56:09.802
eventually WeChat or Weibo,
and things of that nature,

01:56:09.802 --> 01:56:12.179
understand how to be the best
in the world in communicating

01:56:12.179 --> 01:56:14.415
on where the consumer's
spending their time

01:56:14.415 --> 01:56:16.737
in every part of the world.

01:56:16.737 --> 01:56:18.144
- But I guess that's. (background
noise drowns out speaker)

01:56:18.144 --> 01:56:20.797
Right, because, I mean,
unless you have a lot of cash

01:56:20.797 --> 01:56:24.490
to flood into this region, you
have to be a bit more careful

01:56:24.490 --> 01:56:26.524
about your strategy, maybe?

01:56:26.524 --> 01:56:28.433
- Yeah, I mean, I think anybody

01:56:28.433 --> 01:56:31.842
who's running a business
should always have a strategy

01:56:31.842 --> 01:56:34.661
of not losing money, no
matter how much, you know,

01:56:34.661 --> 01:56:36.773
I don't have the luxury
of raising capital.

01:56:36.773 --> 01:56:40.964
So most, many businesses in today's world,

01:56:40.964 --> 01:56:44.923
strategies are very weak,
because they're over-funded,

01:56:44.923 --> 01:56:47.725
and they're losing money,
I don't have that luxury,

01:56:47.725 --> 01:56:49.689
to your point.

01:56:49.689 --> 01:56:50.984
So yeah, so I think it's a good idea

01:56:50.984 --> 01:56:52.949
for businesses not to lose money.

01:56:52.949 --> 01:56:56.286
- So what's your strategy
for the Singapore office?

01:56:56.286 --> 01:56:57.303
- To take advantage of the fact

01:56:57.303 --> 01:56:59.154
that there are very few organizations

01:56:59.154 --> 01:57:03.658
in the entire region that
are doing proper Facebook

01:57:03.658 --> 01:57:08.464
and Google and YouTube media
and creative executions,

01:57:08.464 --> 01:57:11.609
just like basically every
other part of the world.

01:57:11.609 --> 01:57:13.827
- So would you, I mean, that sounds like

01:57:13.827 --> 01:57:16.092
what an agency does, right?
- Yeah.

01:57:16.092 --> 01:57:19.090
- So, I mean, how are you going
to differentiate yourself?

01:57:19.090 --> 01:57:21.992
- By being better at it, it's like--

01:57:21.992 --> 01:57:25.010
- Can you give, like, more specific?

01:57:25.010 --> 01:57:26.672
What do you mean by better?

01:57:26.672 --> 01:57:30.682
- By showing clients that
making their judgment

01:57:30.682 --> 01:57:34.380
on better should not be based
on impressions and likes,

01:57:34.380 --> 01:57:36.702
and arbitrary brand lift studies.

01:57:36.702 --> 01:57:39.985
It should be predicated on
the actual business results

01:57:39.985 --> 01:57:42.787
that the company is trying to achieve.

01:57:42.787 --> 01:57:45.241
That has been our calling card, you know,

01:57:45.241 --> 01:57:48.054
if you are deeply in the agency world,

01:57:48.054 --> 01:57:52.289
the majority of brands in the
world measure success based

01:57:52.289 --> 01:57:55.602
on arbitrary reporting, headlines, awards,

01:57:55.602 --> 01:57:58.472
or internal MMMs that are laughable.

01:57:58.472 --> 01:58:03.139
And what we've been great at
is helping show sales lift,

01:58:04.096 --> 01:58:06.427
or subscription growth,

01:58:06.427 --> 01:58:08.435
or what companies are actually doing.

01:58:08.435 --> 01:58:11.672
And so I don't say it vague, I say it kind

01:58:11.672 --> 01:58:13.418
of the way I think about
everything in life.

01:58:13.418 --> 01:58:15.889
I could say I'm here to swim,

01:58:15.889 --> 01:58:18.107
and then somebody's
gonna win the gold medal

01:58:18.107 --> 01:58:22.582
in 2020 in Tokyo, like, I
really genuinely believe

01:58:22.582 --> 01:58:24.994
what I said, which is I think we're going

01:58:24.994 --> 01:58:29.994
to take clients' money, and
return more business results

01:58:30.038 --> 01:58:32.170
for them than the alternatives.

01:58:32.170 --> 01:58:34.057
- Is that something you
guarantee to clients,

01:58:34.057 --> 01:58:36.378
that if you don't get X results, then--

01:58:36.378 --> 01:58:38.004
- I wish we could, I mean, there are three

01:58:38.004 --> 01:58:39.574
to four scopes we have in the US

01:58:39.574 --> 01:58:42.248
where we control all the
media and all the creative,

01:58:42.248 --> 01:58:44.663
and then we do have profit sharing.

01:58:44.663 --> 01:58:47.370
But the reason we don't do that in 90%

01:58:47.370 --> 01:58:50.127
of scenarios is, unless
we control everything,

01:58:50.127 --> 01:58:52.514
I'm not in a position to, you know,

01:58:52.514 --> 01:58:55.862
guarantee when you don't
control every lever.

01:58:55.862 --> 01:58:59.330
- So in the US, I think I
saw from a Forbes article

01:58:59.330 --> 01:59:03.492
that clients pay a minimum
of a million US dollars,

01:59:03.492 --> 01:59:05.096
I think it's a yearly retainer--

01:59:05.096 --> 01:59:06.449
- Yes, that's right.

01:59:06.449 --> 01:59:08.793
- In Singapore, how much is that gonna be?

01:59:08.793 --> 01:59:11.787
- Zero, and what I mean by that is,

01:59:11.787 --> 01:59:13.230
any time you enter a new market,

01:59:13.230 --> 01:59:15.742
you have to come with enormous
humility and patience.

01:59:15.742 --> 01:59:19.534
And so I don't have a good
enough sense of the budgets

01:59:19.534 --> 01:59:23.513
and the appetite and the full
landscape outside the macro,

01:59:23.513 --> 01:59:27.116
so I don't come in with
requirements in any new market,

01:59:27.116 --> 01:59:30.999
because I think that would be audacious.

01:59:30.999 --> 01:59:34.081
And so obviously, you know,

01:59:34.081 --> 01:59:36.377
what we're doing is more
listening, and having real,

01:59:36.377 --> 01:59:38.246
you know, we're 10 years deep in the US,

01:59:38.246 --> 01:59:40.180
and we understand the landscape.

01:59:40.180 --> 01:59:43.317
Here, my passion is to spend the first 18

01:59:43.317 --> 01:59:47.645
to 24 months listening and
creating ad hoc executions,

01:59:47.645 --> 01:59:49.220
and then getting our feet underneath us

01:59:49.220 --> 01:59:52.295
to understand what the,
what the requirements

01:59:52.295 --> 01:59:55.315
of minimum spend would be, you know,

01:59:55.315 --> 01:59:57.720
three, four, five, six years from now.

01:59:57.720 --> 02:00:00.481
- Okay, but isn't that also a bit risky,

02:00:00.481 --> 02:00:02.648
that you don't really have a range

02:00:02.648 --> 02:00:04.691
for the kind of fees
that you want to charge?

02:00:04.691 --> 02:00:06.997
I mean, it sounds,
like, highly negotiable.

02:00:06.997 --> 02:00:10.781
- It's short-term risky that
you could be unprofitable

02:00:10.781 --> 02:00:13.028
within the region, but for me,

02:00:13.028 --> 02:00:15.222
the macro business can
afford the investment,

02:00:15.222 --> 02:00:17.735
and that's why we're doing it.

02:00:17.735 --> 02:00:21.166
- Okay, I understand that
you also run a VC firm,

02:00:21.166 --> 02:00:23.734
Vayner/RSE, are there any plans to take

02:00:23.734 --> 02:00:26.195
that to Singapore, or Southeast Asia?

02:00:26.195 --> 02:00:29.356
- Not right now, that
fund has been deployed,

02:00:29.356 --> 02:00:30.527
actually, today's a really fun day.

02:00:30.527 --> 02:00:33.080
We sold an incubated
company from the fund,

02:00:33.080 --> 02:00:35.884
Resy, to American Express,
in a large acquisition

02:00:35.884 --> 02:00:39.651
in the US, that fund
has been fully deployed.

02:00:39.651 --> 02:00:44.651
I didn't raise another fund,
so now my investing is just

02:00:44.868 --> 02:00:47.938
my own personal investing,
which I'm not doing a whole lot

02:00:47.938 --> 02:00:51.162
of, 'cause I'm very head down
in operating this business.

02:00:51.162 --> 02:00:54.072
But I am taking and have
been taking meetings

02:00:54.072 --> 02:00:56.886
with startups in the region
because there's a lot

02:00:56.886 --> 02:00:58.449
of interesting stuff going on.

02:00:58.449 --> 02:01:01.008
And so there's no plans
right now, but what's great

02:01:01.008 --> 02:01:03.363
about being an entrepreneur
is, if I decide

02:01:03.363 --> 02:01:05.444
that there's so much
going on here that I need

02:01:05.444 --> 02:01:06.979
to be a part of it, then I would consider

02:01:06.979 --> 02:01:10.270
to grab some LPs, and
maybe raise some capital.

02:01:10.270 --> 02:01:15.051
- How much, or rather, how
big is your fund right now?

02:01:15.051 --> 02:01:17.930
- That fund was deployed, it
was a 25 million dollar fund,

02:01:17.930 --> 02:01:20.229
and so that, all the capital's deployed,

02:01:20.229 --> 02:01:22.392
and so I don't have an
active fund right now,

02:01:22.392 --> 02:01:24.586
but Vayner/RSE was 25 million.

02:01:24.586 --> 02:01:26.931
- And you no plans to raise some more?

02:01:26.931 --> 02:01:28.798
- Not right now, no.
- Okay.

02:01:28.798 --> 02:01:31.633
And I, I mean, your company sell,

02:01:31.633 --> 02:01:34.924
who are your biggest investors--

02:01:34.924 --> 02:01:37.077
- We don't, we don't have any investors.

02:01:37.077 --> 02:01:40.709
We sold a percentage of
the company to Steve Ross,

02:01:40.709 --> 02:01:43.881
the real estate tycoon
and owner of the Dolphins,

02:01:43.881 --> 02:01:46.972
but my brother and I took
that capital off the table.

02:01:46.972 --> 02:01:49.588
So we didn't raise any
capital for the company,

02:01:49.588 --> 02:01:53.879
and if my brother didn't
want money at that time,

02:01:53.879 --> 02:01:56.662
we probably wouldn't have
sold any piece of it.

02:01:56.662 --> 02:01:59.609
And there's no plans to raise any capital.

02:01:59.609 --> 02:02:00.815
- [Maha] Thank you, thank you so much--

02:02:00.815 --> 02:02:01.648
- Gotta run?

02:02:01.648 --> 02:02:02.481
- [Maha] One more question?

02:02:02.481 --> 02:02:03.314
- Just the last one?
- Yep.

02:02:03.314 --> 02:02:05.529
- Okay, so also, actually, in Singapore,

02:02:05.529 --> 02:02:08.300
and most of the global PR industry,

02:02:08.300 --> 02:02:10.118
there's a lot of consolidation happening.

02:02:10.118 --> 02:02:11.170
- Yes.

02:02:11.170 --> 02:02:13.500
- Are there any plans
to perhaps, you know,

02:02:13.500 --> 02:02:15.499
grow your business inorganically

02:02:15.499 --> 02:02:18.010
in Singapore or Southeast Asia?

02:02:18.010 --> 02:02:20.380
- Meaning me doing M&amp;A?
- Yeah.

02:02:20.380 --> 02:02:22.925
- Yeah, I think that's very realistic.

02:02:22.925 --> 02:02:24.175
We've done some

02:02:25.214 --> 02:02:27.494
acquisitions, we bought
a publishing company,

02:02:27.494 --> 02:02:30.151
PureWow, a couple of years ago, and one

02:02:30.151 --> 02:02:31.443
of the things that I am interested

02:02:31.443 --> 02:02:35.178
in is spending time auditing
the landscape of five

02:02:35.178 --> 02:02:39.109
to 50 person shops and
capabilities throughout the region

02:02:39.109 --> 02:02:40.797
because I think there's a ton of talent,

02:02:40.797 --> 02:02:42.114
and some under-priced talent.

02:02:42.114 --> 02:02:46.434
And so I wouldn't, I
wouldn't count that out.

02:02:46.434 --> 02:02:48.819
- So you're looking at,
say, buying another agency?

02:02:48.819 --> 02:02:49.652
- Yes.

02:02:49.652 --> 02:02:51.775
- Okay, any targets right now?

02:02:51.775 --> 02:02:55.015
- No, I haven't even,
couldn't even name one agency

02:02:55.015 --> 02:02:57.422
in any part of Asia.

02:02:57.422 --> 02:03:00.410
- Prior to this, was there
ground research, that

02:03:00.410 --> 02:03:03.768
you send someone to kind of
look at the landscape first,

02:03:03.768 --> 02:03:06.096
or is this just jumping in?

02:03:06.096 --> 02:03:08.106
- This is just jumping
in, I mean, this is,

02:03:08.106 --> 02:03:10.898
the landscape work we've
done is the behavior

02:03:10.898 --> 02:03:14.856
of the biggest brands in the
market on their executions

02:03:14.856 --> 02:03:17.418
on creative and media,
against the platforms

02:03:17.418 --> 02:03:21.020
that we feel are the
most underutilized in,

02:03:21.020 --> 02:03:24.458
you know, and so that's
the homework we've done,

02:03:24.458 --> 02:03:27.708
consumer behavior in the region,
what are consumers doing.

02:03:27.708 --> 02:03:30.996
And are the brands
taking advantage of that

02:03:30.996 --> 02:03:32.571
from a media and creative standpoint,

02:03:32.571 --> 02:03:34.000
and once the decision made was no,

02:03:34.000 --> 02:03:37.388
that's when it was a blind, let's go.

02:03:37.388 --> 02:03:40.388
- And the biggest challenge for you?

02:03:42.211 --> 02:03:43.735
- Health.

02:03:43.735 --> 02:03:47.993
- Oh, sorry, I mean the
Singapore operations.

02:03:47.993 --> 02:03:51.670
- Making sure the people
that run it are healthy.

02:03:51.670 --> 02:03:52.598
- [Maha] Thank you so much.

02:03:52.598 --> 02:03:53.715
- Awesome, thank you.
- Thank you so much.

02:03:53.715 --> 02:03:56.210
- Thank you so much.

02:03:56.210 --> 02:03:57.418
- [Maha] We have one more, Gary.

02:03:57.418 --> 02:03:58.685
(quiet overlapping chattering)

02:03:58.685 --> 02:03:59.768
- Hello.
- Hi.

02:04:01.763 --> 02:04:03.476
But firstly, I think we have to ask you,

02:04:03.476 --> 02:04:07.342
why do you pick Singapore to
set up an office here, why--

02:04:07.342 --> 02:04:09.501
- You know, it's my,
I'm comfortable with it.

02:04:09.501 --> 02:04:13.302
My CFO was a CFO of a large agency here.

02:04:13.302 --> 02:04:15.481
I've enjoyed my trips here, a lot

02:04:15.481 --> 02:04:18.013
of my global clients' offices are here,

02:04:18.013 --> 02:04:20.348
so actually practical,
just very practical.

02:04:20.348 --> 02:04:24.532
A good place to do business,
and a couple serendipitous,

02:04:24.532 --> 02:04:26.240
you know, relationships internally

02:04:26.240 --> 02:04:28.548
that made me feel comfortable.

02:04:28.548 --> 02:04:32.774
- So what's your
impression of Singaporeans

02:04:32.774 --> 02:04:34.805
to work with, in general?

02:04:34.805 --> 02:04:38.850
- Highly disciplined,
pretty entrepreneurial,

02:04:38.850 --> 02:04:41.527
very creative, you know, the executives

02:04:41.527 --> 02:04:43.358
on the big companies
here I think have more

02:04:43.358 --> 02:04:45.683
entrepreneurial spirit than
I've seen in other parts

02:04:45.683 --> 02:04:48.266
of the world, including the US.

02:04:50.533 --> 02:04:52.176
Polite.

02:04:52.176 --> 02:04:53.509
- Oh, wow, okay.

02:04:54.562 --> 02:04:58.834
That's not the term always
used to describe us.

02:04:58.834 --> 02:05:01.548
So you've really created sort
of a cult of personality,

02:05:01.548 --> 02:05:03.970
in the sense, when you
speak across the world,

02:05:03.970 --> 02:05:06.221
people follow you not
just for your business,

02:05:06.221 --> 02:05:07.670
but also for your personality.

02:05:07.670 --> 02:05:11.710
Is that something that you
consciously build up, do you?

02:05:11.710 --> 02:05:15.407
- It's something that I
stumbled into in 2011,

02:05:15.407 --> 02:05:19.490
'12, '13, '14 that now
I'm unbelievably aware of.

02:05:20.586 --> 02:05:23.896
And so I think very, you know, I know

02:05:23.896 --> 02:05:25.346
you're not gonna be able to reference it,

02:05:25.346 --> 02:05:26.794
but I'm sure you're listening.

02:05:26.794 --> 02:05:29.485
For me, things are very macro, you know,

02:05:29.485 --> 02:05:32.050
very, just way up here, then I live,

02:05:32.050 --> 02:05:34.390
then I counterpunch opportunities.

02:05:34.390 --> 02:05:36.628
So I, I mean, I was 34
years old the first time

02:05:36.628 --> 02:05:39.603
I gave my first speech of my
life, it never even crossed

02:05:39.603 --> 02:05:41.987
my mind that anybody on
earth would ever know

02:05:41.987 --> 02:05:44.385
who I was, because I was a businessman.

02:05:44.385 --> 02:05:46.658
Then the internet comes along,
then YouTube comes along,

02:05:46.658 --> 02:05:48.858
then I start making videos,
then I write a book,

02:05:48.858 --> 02:05:51.412
and then all of a sudden
I'm being invited to speak.

02:05:51.412 --> 02:05:53.570
And the first time I give a speech,

02:05:53.570 --> 02:05:55.729
it was, (claps) I was like, oh my god,

02:05:55.729 --> 02:05:58.125
this is, I like this, and I'm good at it.

02:05:58.125 --> 02:06:00.246
And so you start doing it,
and then you start realizing,

02:06:00.246 --> 02:06:02.303
maybe you're really good at it.

02:06:02.303 --> 02:06:03.517
And then you start understanding

02:06:03.517 --> 02:06:05.727
what it means for all your businesses

02:06:05.727 --> 02:06:08.460
when you're building
brand, and so, you know,

02:06:08.460 --> 02:06:10.369
now I'm very aware of what the top

02:06:10.369 --> 02:06:13.158
of the funnel brand awareness I create

02:06:13.158 --> 02:06:14.626
for everything I do,
whether it's sneakers,

02:06:14.626 --> 02:06:16.459
or wine, or my agency.

02:06:17.915 --> 02:06:21.507
So yes, and I, you know, it's interesting.

02:06:21.507 --> 02:06:26.424
I run 1,000 person marketing
communications infrastructure,

02:06:27.358 --> 02:06:30.478
and so it's not so easy for me to speak.

02:06:30.478 --> 02:06:32.516
And so now I try to be very strategic

02:06:32.516 --> 02:06:36.428
about where I speak and why,
but it also is something

02:06:36.428 --> 02:06:38.246
I'm very aware brings a lot of value

02:06:38.246 --> 02:06:40.913
to the overall business ecosystem.

02:06:40.913 --> 02:06:43.255
- But when you're speaking,
you're kind of bold,

02:06:43.255 --> 02:06:45.590
and very straightforward, and very blunt.

02:06:45.590 --> 02:06:47.573
Is that your personality
in real life, as well,

02:06:47.573 --> 02:06:48.564
or is that something--
- Yeah.

02:06:48.564 --> 02:06:50.902
I think I manifest
differently in interviews

02:06:50.902 --> 02:06:52.975
and in videos and onstage than maybe

02:06:52.975 --> 02:06:55.981
who I am as an executive, I think I'm,

02:06:55.981 --> 02:07:00.065
I think I'm more aggressive
when I'm not speaking

02:07:00.065 --> 02:07:04.592
to a single human, and
I'm speaking to humans.

02:07:04.592 --> 02:07:08.484
So I think that's where you see my most

02:07:08.484 --> 02:07:11.647
raw part of me, and then
I think, ironically,

02:07:11.647 --> 02:07:14.514
I'm quite the opposite
way when I'm individual.

02:07:14.514 --> 02:07:17.604
I think my compassion and kind
of my soft skills play out

02:07:17.604 --> 02:07:21.351
when it's a human in front
of me, versus humans.

02:07:21.351 --> 02:07:24.190
So I think there's different
versions of all of us,

02:07:24.190 --> 02:07:27.451
and I think it's not that it's my shtick

02:07:27.451 --> 02:07:32.346
or my persona, it's just,
it's just how I communicate

02:07:32.346 --> 02:07:36.679
when I'm speaking to a
general audience around macro

02:07:38.680 --> 02:07:39.513
things.

02:07:40.598 --> 02:07:42.172
- So you made really a name for yourself

02:07:42.172 --> 02:07:44.508
on social media, and I
know you talked a lot

02:07:44.508 --> 02:07:46.913
about how important social
media is in marketing--

02:07:46.913 --> 02:07:48.158
- Yes.

02:07:48.158 --> 02:07:50.611
- And a lot of people
are making use of it,

02:07:50.611 --> 02:07:51.997
a lot of young people are making use of it

02:07:51.997 --> 02:07:54.905
to become, like,
influencers, travel, food,

02:07:54.905 --> 02:07:57.362
it's very saturated, what
do you think of that?

02:07:57.362 --> 02:07:58.994
Do you think that trend will die out?

02:07:58.994 --> 02:08:00.669
- I don't, I think--
- You don't?

02:08:00.669 --> 02:08:03.689
- No, I do not, I think the
internet infrastructure allows

02:08:03.689 --> 02:08:06.207
for KOLs and influencers at scale.

02:08:06.207 --> 02:08:08.130
I don't think everybody's destined

02:08:08.130 --> 02:08:11.627
to make five million dollars
a year being an influencer,

02:08:11.627 --> 02:08:13.357
but I think what we're gonna learn is,

02:08:13.357 --> 02:08:16.199
there's a very long tail of
it, and I think there's a lot

02:08:16.199 --> 02:08:19.621
of practical lives to be lived that way.

02:08:19.621 --> 02:08:22.681
Today, we view it as somebody
who makes a lot of money,

02:08:22.681 --> 02:08:25.395
probably, to do something very extreme,

02:08:25.395 --> 02:08:27.202
like travel and things of that nature.

02:08:27.202 --> 02:08:30.185
I think what's exciting is
that there's a lot of people

02:08:30.185 --> 02:08:34.874
who make $64,000.00 a year
doing something they don't love,

02:08:34.874 --> 02:08:38.891
and I believe can make 64,000,
or let me use this example,

02:08:38.891 --> 02:08:42.891
59,000, or 48,000 speaking
about shoes, or cars,

02:08:44.709 --> 02:08:49.557
or hair, or wine, and will
have much happier lives

02:08:49.557 --> 02:08:51.797
for it, and so I don't
think we've even begun

02:08:51.797 --> 02:08:56.797
to see the long tail of
personal brands, influencers.

02:08:57.046 --> 02:08:59.602
And I'm excited for humans,
because I think a lot

02:08:59.602 --> 02:09:02.185
of people are gonna be happier.

02:09:03.570 --> 02:09:05.277
- So if you, do you feel that opens

02:09:05.277 --> 02:09:07.490
up a lot more opportunity, in that sense?

02:09:07.490 --> 02:09:08.573
- I do, I do.

02:09:09.605 --> 02:09:11.928
- You've kind of, you know, some people,

02:09:11.928 --> 02:09:14.860
some influencers don't
really have strong content,

02:09:14.860 --> 02:09:17.851
in that sense, it's just photos, and--

02:09:17.851 --> 02:09:19.733
- But, but that's what you think.

02:09:19.733 --> 02:09:20.758
- Okay, okay.

02:09:20.758 --> 02:09:22.780
- You know, and I don't
think I'm right, either.

02:09:22.780 --> 02:09:25.779
If an audience likes it, then
they like it, like, right?

02:09:25.779 --> 02:09:29.761
Like, and so does a
model got good content?

02:09:29.761 --> 02:09:31.898
Like, is somebody who models clothes,

02:09:31.898 --> 02:09:33.510
making four million dollars a year,

02:09:33.510 --> 02:09:37.144
good at what they do, I guess
so, there's a market for it.

02:09:37.144 --> 02:09:40.727
So I don't judge if you're
funny or overly sexy,

02:09:40.727 --> 02:09:42.359
or whatever the hell you're doing.

02:09:42.359 --> 02:09:44.622
If an audience decides that it's good,

02:09:44.622 --> 02:09:48.603
the audience decides, not
me, not you, and I think that

02:09:48.603 --> 02:09:50.763
that is something that
people struggle with

02:09:50.763 --> 02:09:53.051
because we like to impose judgment,

02:09:53.051 --> 02:09:55.841
it is the great flaw of human beings.

02:09:55.841 --> 02:09:59.390
I tend to let the audience decide.

02:09:59.390 --> 02:10:01.651
I genuinely never think I'm right,

02:10:01.651 --> 02:10:04.818
I just think I'm analyzing
an audience at all times.

02:10:04.818 --> 02:10:09.207
And I think too many people
walk around with audacity.

02:10:09.207 --> 02:10:12.358
- Okay, so another thing I
wanted to ask you about is

02:10:12.358 --> 02:10:15.249
because I've heard you
talk a lot about ads,

02:10:15.249 --> 02:10:18.820
and how you think marketing
online is really the way forward

02:10:18.820 --> 02:10:21.289
to go, what do you think about the ethics

02:10:21.289 --> 02:10:25.110
of marketing online, because
people are mining data

02:10:25.110 --> 02:10:26.953
for everything, your credit card history,

02:10:26.953 --> 02:10:29.524
browsing history, voter registration,

02:10:29.524 --> 02:10:31.801
and that can really influence
things like elections,

02:10:31.801 --> 02:10:34.606
for example, what do you
think of the ethics of that?

02:10:34.606 --> 02:10:37.723
- I think humans are capable of,

02:10:37.723 --> 02:10:41.973
of not wanting to be
accountable for their actions.

02:10:42.924 --> 02:10:44.174
So I think that

02:10:45.504 --> 02:10:46.337
ads

02:10:47.359 --> 02:10:50.054
that are targeted are a hell
of a lot better than ads

02:10:50.054 --> 02:10:52.078
that are not targeted, I prefer

02:10:52.078 --> 02:10:54.693
that advertisers have all my information

02:10:54.693 --> 02:10:57.953
because I do not want
to see ads for things

02:10:57.953 --> 02:10:59.922
that I'm not interested in.

02:10:59.922 --> 02:11:00.755
As far as

02:11:01.725 --> 02:11:06.366
elections, those aren't
ads, that is propaganda,

02:11:06.366 --> 02:11:10.088
and content, and humans choose to believe

02:11:10.088 --> 02:11:13.332
what they want to believe,
people go on television

02:11:13.332 --> 02:11:17.340
and newspapers and read the
left and the right, as well.

02:11:17.340 --> 02:11:22.100
So I think it's very lazy
for us to blame Facebook,

02:11:22.100 --> 02:11:25.849
or China, or things of that nature.

02:11:25.849 --> 02:11:27.436
There's a ton of people right now

02:11:27.436 --> 02:11:30.950
that are pumping propaganda
that 5G causes cancer,

02:11:30.950 --> 02:11:32.691
and people choose to believe that,

02:11:32.691 --> 02:11:33.696
or not choose to believe that.

02:11:33.696 --> 02:11:35.771
So I think we're living through an

02:11:35.771 --> 02:11:40.127
incredibly interesting era
where mainstream media is

02:11:40.127 --> 02:11:43.567
desperately scared of
the new media platforms,

02:11:43.567 --> 02:11:45.842
and so they're pumping a lot
of interesting information

02:11:45.842 --> 02:11:48.021
that is molding people's decisions.

02:11:48.021 --> 02:11:51.988
But as for me, I'd prefer
that advertisers had all

02:11:51.988 --> 02:11:54.325
my information, because I'm not worried

02:11:54.325 --> 02:11:57.805
about them knowing about my credit score,

02:11:57.805 --> 02:12:02.138
or my preferences of fruit,
or bicycles, or clothes.

02:12:04.341 --> 02:12:07.127
I think people love to talk about privacy,

02:12:07.127 --> 02:12:09.627
but don't actually believe it.

02:12:10.720 --> 02:12:12.320
- What do you mean by
they don't believe it?

02:12:12.320 --> 02:12:14.189
- I don't think people care about privacy.

02:12:14.189 --> 02:12:15.595
- [Interviewer] Okay.

02:12:15.595 --> 02:12:19.338
- You've got Russia and China
citizens living life knowing

02:12:19.338 --> 02:12:21.565
they have no privacy at the macro,

02:12:21.565 --> 02:12:24.779
you have naive Americans
thinking they did have it,

02:12:24.779 --> 02:12:26.811
and ultimately, nobody gives a crap.

02:12:26.811 --> 02:12:28.265
You're more than welcome
to not put your information

02:12:28.265 --> 02:12:32.293
on the internet, you don't
like it, don't use the apps.

02:12:32.293 --> 02:12:34.866
Instagram's not making you do anything,

02:12:34.866 --> 02:12:36.463
WeChat's not making you do anything.

02:12:36.463 --> 02:12:38.088
People don't want to be accountable

02:12:38.088 --> 02:12:39.919
for their actions, so it's easier

02:12:39.919 --> 02:12:44.481
to blame big internet companies,
versus blaming yourself.

02:12:44.481 --> 02:12:48.572
- Too true, so in the sense,
Singapore audience is a bit,

02:12:48.572 --> 02:12:51.143
you've worked in the US
for, like, 10 years, right?

02:12:51.143 --> 02:12:53.584
But the Singapore audience
is quite different from,

02:12:53.584 --> 02:12:55.842
the demographics is quite different.

02:12:55.842 --> 02:12:58.048
So do you think how you might, you know,

02:12:58.048 --> 02:12:59.606
marketing to Singaporeans, as well,

02:12:59.606 --> 02:13:02.702
some of the concerns that
you suspect you have to--

02:13:02.702 --> 02:13:04.337
- There's no concerns, I think, you know,

02:13:04.337 --> 02:13:05.627
and I think you'll appreciate this.

02:13:05.627 --> 02:13:09.095
There's 17,000 different
Singaporean audiences,

02:13:09.095 --> 02:13:11.464
psychographic, demographic, experiences,

02:13:11.464 --> 02:13:14.204
there's no American audience,
I think the world's starting

02:13:14.204 --> 02:13:16.057
to realize there's two
very different Americas,

02:13:16.057 --> 02:13:17.749
and then there's 50 different Americans

02:13:17.749 --> 02:13:19.823
underneath those two different Americas.

02:13:19.823 --> 02:13:24.101
What's amazing about humans
is they have many truths,

02:13:24.101 --> 02:13:27.542
and then all you have to do
is be culturally relevant.

02:13:27.542 --> 02:13:30.987
So being able to synthesize the pulse

02:13:30.987 --> 02:13:35.987
of a 26-year-old Singaporean
single female takes research

02:13:35.991 --> 02:13:38.413
and development, and testing of creative.

02:13:38.413 --> 02:13:41.234
I don't come in with
opinions, I come in listening

02:13:41.234 --> 02:13:43.877
and reacting, it doesn't take very long,

02:13:43.877 --> 02:13:47.493
if you're willing to listen,
so I go into every market very

02:13:47.493 --> 02:13:50.743
easily, I just listen, and then I talk.

02:13:52.602 --> 02:13:54.151
- So what advice would you have for people

02:13:54.151 --> 02:13:57.462
who are looking to make
it using the media,

02:13:57.462 --> 02:13:59.260
whether they be influencers, or people

02:13:59.260 --> 02:14:02.152
who are using the media to sell something?

02:14:02.152 --> 02:14:04.061
- By trying not to sell something.

02:14:04.061 --> 02:14:05.154
- Oh, okay.

02:14:05.154 --> 02:14:07.344
- I think the biggest way to
actually build an audience

02:14:07.344 --> 02:14:10.653
and create leverage is to
bring value, not extract it.

02:14:10.653 --> 02:14:11.887
And I think one of the biggest reasons

02:14:11.887 --> 02:14:14.309
so many people will not be influencers is

02:14:14.309 --> 02:14:16.525
they are sitting right now, thinking,

02:14:16.525 --> 02:14:18.130
I'm gonna start this page,

02:14:18.130 --> 02:14:20.709
and these people are gonna pay
me, or brands are gonna pay

02:14:20.709 --> 02:14:22.627
me to put something in
front of these people.

02:14:22.627 --> 02:14:24.936
But I haven't brought any
value to these people yet,

02:14:24.936 --> 02:14:27.744
so I don't have any
leverage to put this bottle

